1
00:00:06,065 --> 00:00:08,545
[pensive piano music playing]

2
00:00:10,385 --> 00:00:12,265
[water splashing]

3
00:00:12,345 --> 00:00:14,185
[woman] Three, two,

4
00:00:14,265 --> 00:00:15,825
one, and go!

5
00:00:18,785 --> 00:00:19,785
[whistle blowing]

6
00:00:31,265 --> 00:00:32,505
I win.

7
00:00:32,585 --> 00:00:33,945
Oh, come on, again, please.

8
00:00:34,025 --> 00:00:35,225
[chuckling]

9
00:00:35,785 --> 00:00:36,745
Okay.

10
00:00:37,265 --> 00:00:39,225
I'm totally gonna win this time.

11
00:00:40,985 --> 00:00:42,065
[sighs happily]

12
00:00:42,145 --> 00:00:43,625
[sinister music playing]

13
00:00:43,705 --> 00:00:45,065
[music stops]

14
00:00:45,145 --> 00:00:48,265
20 YEARS LATER

15
00:00:48,345 --> 00:00:49,785
[sighs sadly]

16
00:01:03,305 --> 00:01:05,625
[soft, haunting music]

17
00:01:12,265 --> 00:01:17,545
HONEY, CALL ME. I WANT TO TALK.

18
00:01:21,065 --> 00:01:22,185
[keys jingling]

19
00:01:22,745 --> 00:01:23,865
[car lock beeps]

20
00:01:33,505 --> 00:01:34,905
{\an8}HONEY, CALL ME. I WANT TO TALK.

21
00:01:34,985 --> 00:01:36,945
Lucía, on your phone in class?

22
00:01:37,025 --> 00:01:38,185
[all chuckling]

23
00:01:38,265 --> 00:01:40,145
[school bell rings]

24
00:01:42,225 --> 00:01:43,465
[phone beeps]

25
00:01:43,545 --> 00:01:45,225
Ruth, honey, call me back.

26
00:01:45,865 --> 00:01:46,905
Please.

27
00:01:46,985 --> 00:01:47,985
[phone beeps]

28
00:01:55,505 --> 00:01:57,945
[man on radio] <i>And now</i>
<i>the latest on Alejandro Huelva,</i>

29
00:01:58,025 --> 00:02:01,225
<i>the missing nine-year-old boy</i>
<i>who disappeared while on a bike ride</i>

30
00:02:01,305 --> 00:02:03,105
<i>on October twenty-third.</i>

31
00:02:03,185 --> 00:02:05,625
<i>The search continues,</i>
<i>but remains unsuccessful.</i>

32
00:02:05,705 --> 00:02:08,545
<i>State authorities have been joined</i>
<i>by groups of volunteers,</i>

33
00:02:08,625 --> 00:02:10,705
<i>both local and from neighboring counties,</i>

34
00:02:10,785 --> 00:02:12,865
<i>who have organized</i>
<i>to assist the Civil Guard</i>

35
00:02:12,945 --> 00:02:14,825
<i>- in the ongoing search effort.</i>
- [phone rings]

36
00:02:15,425 --> 00:02:16,865
[woman, faintly] Alejandro!

37
00:02:17,705 --> 00:02:20,585
- [man] Alejandro!
- [woman] Alejandro!

38
00:02:20,665 --> 00:02:22,865
[on radio] <i>If you have information</i>
<i>on the missing boy,</i>

39
00:02:22,945 --> 00:02:27,585
<i>please call the hotline at 984-555-676.</i>

40
00:02:27,665 --> 00:02:30,745
<i>In other news, the fourth quarter</i>
<i>labor report has been released,</i>

41
00:02:30,825 --> 00:02:33,985
<i>the unemployment rate unfortunately is…</i>

42
00:02:35,065 --> 00:02:37,065
[haunting music playing]

43
00:02:38,865 --> 00:02:40,865
[indistinct chatter]

44
00:02:45,625 --> 00:02:48,425
{\an8}[music intensifies]

45
00:03:13,185 --> 00:03:18,105
THE TRUTHERS

46
00:03:18,185 --> 00:03:20,745
["Amante Bandido" by Miguel Bosé
playing on radio]

47
00:03:24,865 --> 00:03:26,065
[music stops]

48
00:03:28,785 --> 00:03:30,625
[dog barking]

49
00:03:39,665 --> 00:03:40,665
[phone beeps]

50
00:03:41,425 --> 00:03:42,905
[line ringing]

51
00:03:45,625 --> 00:03:48,345
- [dance pop music playing loudly]
- [phone ringing]

52
00:03:51,145 --> 00:03:52,185
[phone clicks]

53
00:04:01,545 --> 00:04:02,825
Hey, are you okay?

54
00:04:03,385 --> 00:04:04,305
Yeah, I'm fine.

55
00:04:05,625 --> 00:04:07,705
- I know what you need.
- No.

56
00:04:07,785 --> 00:04:09,585
We're not going out tonight.

57
00:04:10,305 --> 00:04:12,065
Okay, whatever.

58
00:04:13,345 --> 00:04:14,385
- Cheers!
- Cheers!

59
00:04:14,465 --> 00:04:15,705
[techno music playing]

60
00:04:16,505 --> 00:04:18,345
- [cellphone chimes]
- Whoo!

61
00:04:23,305 --> 00:04:26,065
MOM: RUTH, PLEASE CALL ME.
IT'S IMPORTANT

62
00:04:26,145 --> 00:04:27,465
All good?

63
00:04:28,585 --> 00:04:31,385
Yeah. Two more!

64
00:04:31,465 --> 00:04:33,145
[techno music continues]

65
00:04:41,945 --> 00:04:43,345
[music stops]

66
00:04:43,425 --> 00:04:45,185
- You look great.
- Oh, no.

67
00:04:45,265 --> 00:04:47,345
- Yes, you do. [chuckles]
- Fuck!

68
00:04:48,105 --> 00:04:49,265
What time is it?

69
00:04:49,345 --> 00:04:52,065
Yeah, it's bright outside,
so I don't know.

70
00:04:52,145 --> 00:04:55,785
- It's… seven? Eight?
- This wasn't supposed to happen, Rose.

71
00:04:55,865 --> 00:04:57,105
I could have a coffee now.

72
00:04:57,185 --> 00:04:58,985
<i>"Un Americano con leche, por favor."</i>

73
00:04:59,065 --> 00:05:00,585
- Is that how you say it?
- Yes.

74
00:05:00,665 --> 00:05:02,265
- Okay.
- [cell phone buzzing]

75
00:05:02,345 --> 00:05:04,185
Oh fuck.

76
00:05:04,265 --> 00:05:06,425
- I'm gonna get something to eat.
- Uh, yeah.

77
00:05:11,665 --> 00:05:15,385
- What's the matter?
- [man] <i>Uh… Ruth, it… it's Mom.</i>

78
00:05:15,465 --> 00:05:16,385
What?

79
00:05:16,465 --> 00:05:17,585
<i>Your mom fell.</i>

80
00:05:17,665 --> 00:05:19,465
You mean… What do you mean?

81
00:05:20,785 --> 00:05:22,145
Is she okay, or…

82
00:05:23,625 --> 00:05:25,105
<i>Mom is dead, Ruth.</i>

83
00:05:25,185 --> 00:05:27,665
[somber music playing]

84
00:05:59,265 --> 00:06:04,345
[man on PA] <i>Iberia flight 8363</i>
<i>to Madrid is now boarding.</i>

85
00:06:04,425 --> 00:06:07,865
Passengers, please proceed
to gate number B69.

86
00:06:07,945 --> 00:06:09,345
URGENT MISSING

87
00:06:09,425 --> 00:06:12,065
<i>Please proceed to gate B…</i>

88
00:06:12,705 --> 00:06:13,945
[train horn blares]

89
00:06:15,945 --> 00:06:18,465
[woman on radio] <i>He went out</i>
<i>on his bike every day after school,</i>

90
00:06:18,545 --> 00:06:21,385
<i>but he was gone for hours</i>
<i>and didn't come home for dinner.</i>

91
00:06:21,465 --> 00:06:24,225
<i>And he still hasn't come home.</i>
<i>We know he's out there.</i>

92
00:06:24,305 --> 00:06:26,585
<i>If anybody sees him, please…</i>

93
00:06:26,665 --> 00:06:28,585
If he's been gone for this long…

94
00:06:28,665 --> 00:06:29,825
[clicks teeth]

95
00:06:29,905 --> 00:06:32,265
It's awful. The whole town is in shock.

96
00:06:34,505 --> 00:06:36,305
They're out there looking every day,

97
00:06:36,385 --> 00:06:38,385
but I don't think there's much hope.

98
00:06:38,465 --> 00:06:40,705
[somber music continues]

99
00:07:03,785 --> 00:07:05,905
[music fades]

100
00:07:07,425 --> 00:07:08,985
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

101
00:07:09,945 --> 00:07:11,025
[car door slams]

102
00:07:19,265 --> 00:07:20,265
What happened?

103
00:07:21,185 --> 00:07:22,345
[sighs]

104
00:07:25,345 --> 00:07:26,625
We need to be strong.

105
00:07:29,425 --> 00:07:30,705
Especially me.

106
00:07:34,185 --> 00:07:35,745
Why's the Civil Guard here?

107
00:07:36,545 --> 00:07:39,665
[camera clicking]

108
00:07:43,785 --> 00:07:45,185
Okay. Thanks, Carlos.

109
00:07:48,705 --> 00:07:52,785
Look, I'm sorry, but… even though
it looks like it was an accident,

110
00:07:52,865 --> 00:07:54,785
we still have to write a report.

111
00:07:55,825 --> 00:07:57,225
So what happened?

112
00:07:58,505 --> 00:08:00,825
Mm. She slipped in the shower.

113
00:08:01,745 --> 00:08:04,585
It looks as though
she had been drinking a bit.

114
00:08:07,905 --> 00:08:09,345
Uh… impossible.

115
00:08:10,505 --> 00:08:11,745
What are you saying?

116
00:08:11,825 --> 00:08:12,945
I mean she, she never…

117
00:08:13,025 --> 00:08:14,585
My mother had stopped drinking.

118
00:08:14,665 --> 00:08:16,465
She'd been drinking again.

119
00:08:16,545 --> 00:08:19,265
[moody music playing]

120
00:08:19,345 --> 00:08:20,505
She drank.

121
00:08:21,065 --> 00:08:24,425
[officer] Ruth, it seems that your mother,
around midnight, went upstairs

122
00:08:24,505 --> 00:08:26,945
and into the bathroom to take a shower.

123
00:08:27,945 --> 00:08:29,065
Shall we go up?

124
00:08:29,145 --> 00:08:30,065
Of course.

125
00:08:34,785 --> 00:08:36,865
[officer] Your father
was upstairs, in the attic,

126
00:08:36,945 --> 00:08:39,985
and noticed that your mother
was in the shower a long time.

127
00:08:40,065 --> 00:08:44,265
[father] I asked if she was all right. She
didn't answer. I could hear water running.

128
00:08:44,345 --> 00:08:46,185
I had to break down the door.

129
00:08:50,065 --> 00:08:51,105
She was here.

130
00:08:52,265 --> 00:08:54,865
Not moving. She… she wasn't breathing.

131
00:08:54,945 --> 00:08:57,105
[officer] We presume
that when she slipped,

132
00:08:57,185 --> 00:09:00,225
she fell into the shower screen
and it broke.

133
00:09:00,305 --> 00:09:03,465
The impact when
her head hit the floor, it, well…

134
00:09:03,985 --> 00:09:05,625
She died quickly.

135
00:09:09,385 --> 00:09:12,105
Please, you can't really touch anything.
For the moment, sorry.

136
00:09:12,705 --> 00:09:16,465
Sorry, it's just that my mother
never used the bolt on the doors.

137
00:09:16,545 --> 00:09:18,025
She, she didn't like it.

138
00:09:18,105 --> 00:09:20,185
She never locked a single
door in this house.

139
00:09:20,265 --> 00:09:23,025
- Anyone locked a door, she'd yell at us.
- Honey.

140
00:09:24,505 --> 00:09:27,465
I mean, It's like we're talking
about a whole different person.

141
00:09:28,465 --> 00:09:29,865
Ruth.

142
00:09:30,545 --> 00:09:31,745
I don't recognize her.

143
00:09:32,625 --> 00:09:35,265
It's over two years
since you last came to visit us.

144
00:09:36,225 --> 00:09:38,065
I'd be surprised if you recognized her.

145
00:09:41,865 --> 00:09:44,825
[officer] Now we just have to wait,
there's a ton of paperwork,

146
00:09:44,905 --> 00:09:46,905
- mostly tedious stuff.
- [moody music fades]

147
00:09:46,985 --> 00:09:48,905
- [father] Patience.
- Patience. That's it.

148
00:09:48,985 --> 00:09:50,185
Of course.

149
00:09:50,265 --> 00:09:52,065
I was giving your father the update.

150
00:09:52,145 --> 00:09:56,385
That we still have, uh, paperwork,
routine checks to do, you know.

151
00:09:57,345 --> 00:09:59,785
But once we have
the toxicology report back,

152
00:09:59,865 --> 00:10:01,665
if everything looks all right,

153
00:10:01,745 --> 00:10:04,585
we'll release the body and you
can have her back for the funeral,

154
00:10:04,665 --> 00:10:06,185
the wake, everything else.

155
00:10:06,265 --> 00:10:07,865
Hopefully, it won't be too long.

156
00:10:07,945 --> 00:10:10,745
In the meantime,
if you need anything at all.

157
00:10:13,865 --> 00:10:14,865
Come on.

158
00:10:15,385 --> 00:10:18,745
- Martín, you know how to find me.
- Thanks again.

159
00:10:18,825 --> 00:10:21,145
- No problem. Bye for now.
- Take care.

160
00:10:22,585 --> 00:10:24,585
[intriguing music playing]

161
00:10:43,425 --> 00:10:44,625
[music fades]

162
00:10:44,705 --> 00:10:47,665
Well, if you need anything,
you know where I am.

163
00:10:50,625 --> 00:10:51,625
Dad.

164
00:10:55,985 --> 00:10:57,625
Did something happen yesterday?

165
00:11:00,545 --> 00:11:01,825
What are you asking?

166
00:11:02,905 --> 00:11:04,265
Well, Mom called me.

167
00:11:05,825 --> 00:11:06,905
Many times.

168
00:11:06,985 --> 00:11:09,785
And I was having a bad day
and I didn't answer.

169
00:11:10,385 --> 00:11:13,105
And messages, too, on my voicemail.

170
00:11:13,185 --> 00:11:14,705
Well, what did she say?

171
00:11:17,865 --> 00:11:18,985
That we had to talk.

172
00:11:21,265 --> 00:11:22,745
We had a bit of an argument.

173
00:11:24,065 --> 00:11:25,105
About what?

174
00:11:27,545 --> 00:11:29,825
It was about how much she was drinking.

175
00:11:31,145 --> 00:11:33,745
I think that's why
she locked the bathroom door.

176
00:11:33,825 --> 00:11:37,105
Maybe she wanted to keep drinking
without me saying anything.

177
00:11:38,025 --> 00:11:39,625
But her glass was downstairs.

178
00:11:39,705 --> 00:11:40,585
Huh?

179
00:11:41,305 --> 00:11:42,545
Her glass, she…

180
00:11:42,625 --> 00:11:44,825
She didn't take it with her,
it was downstairs.

181
00:11:45,865 --> 00:11:47,905
She was drunk. Maybe she forgot it.

182
00:11:49,065 --> 00:11:50,065
Does it matter?

183
00:11:51,585 --> 00:11:53,705
It does. It's important.

184
00:11:53,785 --> 00:11:57,305
It matters because she called me
and I didn't answer, and…

185
00:11:57,385 --> 00:12:00,065
And I was going
to call her today, and now…

186
00:12:00,145 --> 00:12:02,145
[emotional music playing]

187
00:12:02,745 --> 00:12:03,945
[sobbing]

188
00:12:13,865 --> 00:12:15,305
[distant barking]

189
00:12:41,385 --> 00:12:42,465
[music fades]

190
00:12:42,545 --> 00:12:43,665
[sighing]

191
00:12:52,065 --> 00:12:55,225
[mother] <i>Ruth, are you</i>
<i>really not going to pick up?</i>

192
00:12:55,305 --> 00:12:58,985
<i>God knows what you're doing there,</i>
<i>running around in Berlin, really.</i>

193
00:12:59,065 --> 00:13:01,305
<i>But it's your life,</i>
<i>you do what you need to.</i>

194
00:13:01,385 --> 00:13:03,945
<i>You're always blaming me,</i>
<i>saying I'm trying to control you.</i>

195
00:13:04,025 --> 00:13:05,745
<i>But, no.</i>

196
00:13:06,825 --> 00:13:08,345
<i>This is important.</i>

197
00:13:08,425 --> 00:13:10,785
<i>Please call me back.</i>

198
00:13:10,865 --> 00:13:12,585
<i>It's about your father, Ruth.</i>

199
00:13:12,665 --> 00:13:14,825
<i>- Your father isn't…</i>
- [intriguing music playing]

200
00:13:14,905 --> 00:13:17,785
<i>He's not the person he used to be.</i>
<i>He's different.</i>

201
00:13:17,865 --> 00:13:19,225
<i>For a while now </i>[sighs]<i>…</i>

202
00:13:19,305 --> 00:13:23,105
<i>I didn't wanna say anything, I didn't</i>
<i>wanna worry you. But the truth is…</i>

203
00:13:24,145 --> 00:13:25,185
[mute chime]

204
00:13:25,265 --> 00:13:26,225
[unmutes]

205
00:13:26,305 --> 00:13:29,625
<i>I'm sorry. I thought I heard him coming.</i>

206
00:13:29,705 --> 00:13:30,865
<i>Ruth.</i>

207
00:13:30,945 --> 00:13:33,665
<i>Listen. Your father…</i>

208
00:13:33,745 --> 00:13:37,025
<i>Your… your father, he's scaring me.</i>

209
00:13:37,585 --> 00:13:41,505
<i>Ever since he retired, he spends all day</i>
<i>locked away in the attic.</i>

210
00:13:41,585 --> 00:13:43,065
<i>He's changed so much.</i>

211
00:13:43,145 --> 00:13:45,825
<i>And, I don't know</i>
<i>how to explain it to you, but…</i>

212
00:13:46,705 --> 00:13:49,745
<i>But I'm afraid one of these days</i>
<i>he's gonna do something crazy.</i>

213
00:13:49,825 --> 00:13:51,305
<i>Call me, please.</i>

214
00:13:52,065 --> 00:13:53,065
<i>I love you.</i>

215
00:13:53,145 --> 00:13:55,145
[intriguing music continues]

216
00:14:07,985 --> 00:14:11,025
[music becomes tense]

217
00:14:13,865 --> 00:14:15,545
[door creaking, shuts]

218
00:14:16,625 --> 00:14:18,545
[lock clicking]

219
00:14:32,265 --> 00:14:35,745
CIVIL GUARD - DO NOT ENTER

220
00:14:43,825 --> 00:14:46,025
[tense music continues]

221
00:14:54,025 --> 00:14:55,585
- [knocking]
- [music stops]

222
00:14:55,665 --> 00:14:56,705
Dad?

223
00:15:06,345 --> 00:15:07,665
[door knob rattles]

224
00:15:19,585 --> 00:15:21,705
Mm. Did you sleep well?

225
00:15:22,505 --> 00:15:25,585
- [plate clatters]
- Not overdone, just the way you like 'em.

226
00:15:28,465 --> 00:15:30,065
- There you go.
- Thank you.

227
00:15:32,265 --> 00:15:33,505
You want anything else?

228
00:15:33,585 --> 00:15:36,225
- Some orange juice?
- No, I'm not that hungry.

229
00:15:37,865 --> 00:15:39,625
Can I help with anything today?

230
00:15:40,265 --> 00:15:41,705
Hm, no, don't worry about it.

231
00:15:41,785 --> 00:15:44,745
We're still waiting on the okay
from the sergeant, for now.

232
00:15:45,345 --> 00:15:46,945
If you want, I can do the shopping or…

233
00:15:47,745 --> 00:15:49,545
Or maybe I can help with the greenhouse.

234
00:15:49,625 --> 00:15:53,145
Nah, I stopped taking care of
that thing a few months ago.

235
00:15:55,505 --> 00:15:56,505
Why's that?

236
00:15:57,145 --> 00:15:58,625
I've just been too busy.

237
00:16:00,505 --> 00:16:02,105
You're retired, Dad.

238
00:16:03,265 --> 00:16:05,425
And I'm just supposed
to sit around gardening all day

239
00:16:05,505 --> 00:16:06,745
now that I'm retired?

240
00:16:07,385 --> 00:16:08,385
Eat, please.

241
00:16:13,105 --> 00:16:15,425
Last night I heard your mother's voice.

242
00:16:16,865 --> 00:16:18,225
In a dream or…?

243
00:16:18,305 --> 00:16:21,785
No. On your phone, it sounded like.

244
00:16:21,865 --> 00:16:23,225
[moody music playing]

245
00:16:23,305 --> 00:16:24,625
She left you a message?

246
00:16:26,505 --> 00:16:27,505
No.

247
00:16:27,585 --> 00:16:29,705
They were just some old voice notes.

248
00:16:30,225 --> 00:16:31,265
You're sure?

249
00:16:32,545 --> 00:16:33,545
Why?

250
00:16:34,345 --> 00:16:37,225
Because I'm your dad, honey.
I know when you lie to me.

251
00:16:37,905 --> 00:16:41,945
Maybe you could try using that instinct
to know when I'm not feeling like talking.

252
00:16:42,025 --> 00:16:44,185
Why can't you tell me
what she said to you?

253
00:16:44,265 --> 00:16:47,345
- Because it was between me and Mom.
- Yeah, sure, like always.

254
00:16:48,065 --> 00:16:50,825
God forbid I should ever
set foot in your world.

255
00:16:51,905 --> 00:16:54,065
You and your mother
constantly at each other's throats.

256
00:16:54,145 --> 00:16:57,065
If I ever tried to intervene,
you'd shut down, you'd both shut down.

257
00:16:57,145 --> 00:16:59,345
Well, it's really not that big a deal.

258
00:16:59,425 --> 00:17:01,305
That's over now, sweetheart.

259
00:17:02,545 --> 00:17:05,985
We can't afford
to have secrets anymore, okay?

260
00:17:06,505 --> 00:17:09,065
Your mother is gone, it's just us now.

261
00:17:10,985 --> 00:17:12,065
Yeah, okay.

262
00:17:12,145 --> 00:17:13,825
[birds chirping]

263
00:17:19,665 --> 00:17:22,505
[mysterious music playing]

264
00:17:42,705 --> 00:17:43,745
[father] Ruth.

265
00:17:47,065 --> 00:17:49,065
The Civil Guard sergeant called.

266
00:17:50,145 --> 00:17:53,465
The coroner is all finished.
We can go and pick up your mother.

267
00:17:54,465 --> 00:17:56,345
And they didn't find anything odd?

268
00:17:57,865 --> 00:17:59,345
What would they find?

269
00:18:00,185 --> 00:18:02,305
I mean, she drank, she fell.

270
00:18:04,545 --> 00:18:07,145
[objects rustling, banging]

271
00:18:07,225 --> 00:18:08,625
[music stops]

272
00:18:13,305 --> 00:18:14,385
Can I help?

273
00:18:14,465 --> 00:18:16,425
Uh, no, it's okay, honey.

274
00:18:17,825 --> 00:18:20,305
Hey! Come on. I can do that.

275
00:18:20,385 --> 00:18:22,425
I told you, it's okay.

276
00:18:22,505 --> 00:18:23,465
Here. Give me that.

277
00:18:24,745 --> 00:18:27,185
Let's have a race, we'll see who wins.

278
00:18:27,265 --> 00:18:28,825
We could swim for it.

279
00:18:29,385 --> 00:18:30,985
Hey, that's no fair.

280
00:18:31,745 --> 00:18:33,745
I'm no mermaid out in the water.

281
00:18:33,825 --> 00:18:35,145
Unlike you, honey.

282
00:18:35,785 --> 00:18:36,945
[Ruth] Mm.

283
00:18:40,025 --> 00:18:42,665
You'd have a chance,
this mermaid's a little out of practice.

284
00:18:44,105 --> 00:18:45,305
[object thuds]

285
00:18:46,625 --> 00:18:47,705
What are you doing?

286
00:18:48,385 --> 00:18:50,465
Uh, making my way through this garbage.

287
00:18:52,385 --> 00:18:53,905
Those are Mom's face creams.

288
00:18:53,985 --> 00:18:55,105
Garbage.

289
00:18:55,185 --> 00:18:56,705
- [object thuds]
- Poison.

290
00:18:56,785 --> 00:18:59,505
And those motherfuckers
charge a fortune for 'em.

291
00:18:59,585 --> 00:19:00,785
[object thuds]

292
00:19:00,865 --> 00:19:02,865
Since when do you swear this much?

293
00:19:03,385 --> 00:19:07,225
You realize those bastards
are ruining people's lives?

294
00:19:07,305 --> 00:19:09,505
They're bleeding out every last dollar.

295
00:19:11,145 --> 00:19:12,625
Who's bleeding people, Dad?

296
00:19:14,705 --> 00:19:16,505
The giant pharma companies.

297
00:19:17,625 --> 00:19:19,905
You know what
they've done in America, right?

298
00:19:20,465 --> 00:19:23,505
They have half the country
hooked on opioids,

299
00:19:23,585 --> 00:19:25,185
not to mention the fentanyl.

300
00:19:25,265 --> 00:19:28,865
They drug you, bankrupt you,
and turn you into a zombie.

301
00:19:30,185 --> 00:19:31,025
[thuds]

302
00:19:31,105 --> 00:19:33,665
What's that have to do with
the products Mom bought?

303
00:19:33,745 --> 00:19:35,225
Do you know how they make these?

304
00:19:35,305 --> 00:19:37,345
Experimenting on animals.

305
00:19:38,105 --> 00:19:39,025
Yeah.

306
00:19:39,665 --> 00:19:40,825
And people too.

307
00:19:42,625 --> 00:19:43,545
People?

308
00:19:43,625 --> 00:19:44,945
On children.

309
00:19:46,145 --> 00:19:48,745
Children like Alejandro.
Haven't you heard?

310
00:19:48,825 --> 00:19:50,145
[dramatic music playing]

311
00:19:50,225 --> 00:19:52,225
It's impossible for anyone to miss.

312
00:19:53,425 --> 00:19:55,385
He vanished into thin air.

313
00:19:56,025 --> 00:19:58,505
And guess who just came
to town and set up shop?

314
00:19:59,865 --> 00:20:02,705
The headquarters of a multinational
pharmaceutical company.

315
00:20:02,785 --> 00:20:03,745
Coincidence?

316
00:20:04,745 --> 00:20:06,905
No such thing as a coincidence, honey.

317
00:20:08,065 --> 00:20:09,985
There are some links I can send you.

318
00:20:10,065 --> 00:20:12,945
There are millions of videos, of articles.

319
00:20:13,025 --> 00:20:15,825
- People fighting to bring this to light.
- Ah, c'mon, Dad.

320
00:20:16,505 --> 00:20:17,505
Yeah.

321
00:20:18,265 --> 00:20:19,945
I don't know why I'm surprised.

322
00:20:20,985 --> 00:20:22,665
Exactly like your mother.

323
00:20:25,585 --> 00:20:26,585
[object clatters]

324
00:20:29,265 --> 00:20:30,585
[music fades]

325
00:20:30,665 --> 00:20:34,105
Hello, Sergeant, how are you?
It's Ruth, Martín's daughter.

326
00:20:34,905 --> 00:20:38,545
My… my dad already told me
that you got the coroner's report back

327
00:20:38,625 --> 00:20:40,185
and it says it was an accident.

328
00:20:40,265 --> 00:20:41,505
[sergeant] <i>That's right.</i>

329
00:20:41,585 --> 00:20:43,705
Yes. Well, I…

330
00:20:43,785 --> 00:20:46,865
Tell me if I'm out of line or…
or if it's okay to ask,

331
00:20:46,945 --> 00:20:49,025
but I'd like to know if…

332
00:20:50,065 --> 00:20:54,425
If it's possible for me
to see the report.

333
00:20:54,505 --> 00:20:55,545
Or…

334
00:20:56,265 --> 00:20:59,105
Or the photos or… or something, because…

335
00:20:59,585 --> 00:21:02,185
Well, it's just that I'd like…
I'd like to…

336
00:21:02,265 --> 00:21:03,745
know exactly what…

337
00:21:04,465 --> 00:21:05,745
what they think happened.

338
00:21:05,825 --> 00:21:06,825
And…

339
00:21:08,305 --> 00:21:09,265
Hello?

340
00:21:10,385 --> 00:21:12,585
<i>You wanna see a copy of the report?</i>

341
00:21:13,665 --> 00:21:17,425
Yes, if it's possible,
yes, I'd like to see it.

342
00:21:18,825 --> 00:21:19,825
<i>No problem.</i>

343
00:21:20,465 --> 00:21:21,465
Great.

344
00:21:21,545 --> 00:21:23,705
[mysterious music playing]

345
00:21:24,705 --> 00:21:25,905
[call disconnects]

346
00:21:40,945 --> 00:21:44,185
[podcast host] <i>This is a phenomenon</i>
<i>that's been going on for decades,</i>

347
00:21:44,265 --> 00:21:47,145
<i>and only recently has enough</i>
<i>evidence come to light.</i>

348
00:21:47,225 --> 00:21:50,265
<i>And all of it has been happening</i>
<i>right under our noses,</i>

349
00:21:50,345 --> 00:21:53,105
<i>across the world in every</i>
<i>first world country.</i>

350
00:21:53,185 --> 00:21:56,225
<i>It's vital we spread the word</i>
<i>to protect our children at all costs.</i>

351
00:21:56,305 --> 00:21:57,305
WAKE UP

352
00:21:57,985 --> 00:22:00,585
[host 1] <i>In Spain alone,</i>
<i>between one and two hundred children</i>

353
00:22:00,665 --> 00:22:02,305
<i>go missing each year.</i>

354
00:22:02,385 --> 00:22:07,305
<i>I'm sure our listeners are wondering</i>
<i>who would kidnap these kids and why.</i>

355
00:22:07,385 --> 00:22:09,345
[host 2] <i>The reason is adrenochrome,</i>

356
00:22:09,425 --> 00:22:12,345
<i>a chemical compound that children</i>
<i>produce when under duress,</i>

357
00:22:12,425 --> 00:22:14,585
<i>but which has</i>
<i>restorative effects on adults.</i>

358
00:22:14,665 --> 00:22:15,945
{\an8}THE NEW CONSPIRACY THEORISTS

359
00:22:16,025 --> 00:22:19,025
[host 1] <i>Are we talking about</i>
<i>kidnapping children to scare them?</i>

360
00:22:19,105 --> 00:22:22,705
[host 1] <i>To terrorize, to torture them,</i>
<i>the worst atrocities imaginable.</i>

361
00:22:22,785 --> 00:22:24,345
CONSPIRACY THEORIES HAVE EVOLVED

362
00:22:24,425 --> 00:22:27,905
<i>- And when they stop being useful?</i>
- [host 2] <i>They kill them, of course.</i>

363
00:22:27,985 --> 00:22:30,905
<i>The number of victims in just</i>
<i>the past few years is incalculable.</i>

364
00:22:30,985 --> 00:22:33,545
<i>But mainstream media</i>
<i>won't tell you the truth.</i>

365
00:22:33,625 --> 00:22:36,585
[host 1] <i>No, the media don't want</i>
<i>the truth coming out.</i>

366
00:22:36,665 --> 00:22:39,545
[melancholy piano music playing]

367
00:22:55,185 --> 00:22:57,185
[people chattering]

368
00:22:58,065 --> 00:22:59,665
- I'm sorry.
- Mm.

369
00:23:06,825 --> 00:23:07,825
Hi, Grandma.

370
00:23:07,905 --> 00:23:09,065
Hello.

371
00:23:11,585 --> 00:23:13,065
Hi, honey. How are you?

372
00:23:17,985 --> 00:23:19,345
Such a shame, dear.

373
00:23:20,305 --> 00:23:21,745
How's Martín been?

374
00:23:21,825 --> 00:23:22,665
Mm…

375
00:23:22,745 --> 00:23:24,945
I dunno. I think we're all in shock.

376
00:23:30,465 --> 00:23:32,345
It's been a lot, I guess.

377
00:23:48,225 --> 00:23:49,425
Oh, thank you.

378
00:23:50,825 --> 00:23:52,425
I haven't eaten in two days.

379
00:23:53,665 --> 00:23:56,625
I keep seeing your mother
lying on the floor and, uh…

380
00:23:57,625 --> 00:24:00,505
I don't understand
how it could've happened. I don't get it.

381
00:24:02,105 --> 00:24:03,345
Neither do I.

382
00:24:04,705 --> 00:24:06,345
You heard she'd been drinking?

383
00:24:08,105 --> 00:24:09,025
Yes.

384
00:24:09,945 --> 00:24:11,905
Look, your mother wasn't doing well.

385
00:24:11,985 --> 00:24:15,665
She was feeling really down, sad.

386
00:24:17,065 --> 00:24:18,185
I had no idea.

387
00:24:19,545 --> 00:24:23,265
I know, and it's so weird
for me to be talking about her like this.

388
00:24:23,345 --> 00:24:24,945
Especially in the past tense.

389
00:24:27,465 --> 00:24:29,025
Did she drink because of my dad?

390
00:24:29,105 --> 00:24:30,345
Martín, he's…

391
00:24:30,425 --> 00:24:33,705
I don't wanna get started about your dad,
because he got weird.

392
00:24:33,785 --> 00:24:35,545
I don't know. Retirement?

393
00:24:36,225 --> 00:24:38,705
I don't think that he's adjusting.

394
00:24:38,785 --> 00:24:42,145
I mean, and the pandemic,
that was hard on all of us.

395
00:24:42,225 --> 00:24:46,585
Did you know we had to take
your mother to get vaccinated in secret?

396
00:24:47,625 --> 00:24:49,185
She was so worried about him.

397
00:24:49,865 --> 00:24:51,465
That he would find out.

398
00:24:52,145 --> 00:24:55,265
She didn't know how he would
take it and then… Bah!

399
00:24:58,025 --> 00:24:59,065
But do…

400
00:25:01,625 --> 00:25:03,145
I don't know, do you think…

401
00:25:04,385 --> 00:25:07,185
When they fought that he got
violent or anything like that?

402
00:25:07,265 --> 00:25:08,225
That he…

403
00:25:08,745 --> 00:25:11,745
I don't really know,
because your mother, you know she…

404
00:25:11,825 --> 00:25:14,785
She wouldn't talk about it.
Not to me, not to anyone.

405
00:25:34,105 --> 00:25:36,865
[intriguing music playing]

406
00:25:38,185 --> 00:25:40,785
Okay, here it is. The copy of the report.

407
00:25:41,745 --> 00:25:44,265
There are some pictures in there
from when we found her.

408
00:25:44,345 --> 00:25:46,345
I wouldn't look, if I were you.

409
00:25:48,025 --> 00:25:50,025
[intriguing music continues]

410
00:26:08,945 --> 00:26:13,105
PROBABLE CAUSE OF DEATH:
SEVERE HEAD TRAUMA FROM ACCIDENTAL FALL

411
00:26:42,545 --> 00:26:44,465
[music fades]

412
00:26:57,825 --> 00:26:58,865
Ruth…

413
00:26:58,945 --> 00:27:00,745
Oh, it's so terrible.

414
00:27:01,785 --> 00:27:03,825
Our deepest condolences.

415
00:27:05,065 --> 00:27:06,945
She worked us so hard.

416
00:27:07,465 --> 00:27:08,825
All day swimming laps.

417
00:27:08,905 --> 00:27:11,705
Well, she was very strict.
But such a great coach.

418
00:27:11,785 --> 00:27:12,745
Yeah.

419
00:27:12,825 --> 00:27:15,145
[woman] She missed you so much. Well…

420
00:27:15,825 --> 00:27:18,185
Sorry, but do you know who that is,
the group with my dad?

421
00:27:19,305 --> 00:27:21,465
Them? Oh, that's his search party.

422
00:27:23,185 --> 00:27:24,225
His search party?

423
00:27:25,305 --> 00:27:29,065
I'm not sure if you've heard,
but there's a missing child, and, well…

424
00:27:29,145 --> 00:27:31,545
The neighbors have organized
these groups to look for him.

425
00:27:31,625 --> 00:27:33,705
That's Mariano, the new commissioner.

426
00:27:33,785 --> 00:27:35,825
Luis, from the garage.

427
00:27:35,905 --> 00:27:38,665
- And that's Alfonso, the police doctor.
- [intriguing music playing]

428
00:27:39,305 --> 00:27:40,505
I'm sorry?

429
00:27:41,385 --> 00:27:42,905
You know, I mean the…

430
00:27:43,665 --> 00:27:45,025
Oh, what do you call it?

431
00:27:45,105 --> 00:27:46,025
The coroner.

432
00:27:47,105 --> 00:27:48,145
Coroner.

433
00:27:49,065 --> 00:27:50,145
The coroner.

434
00:27:54,425 --> 00:27:56,585
- Excuse me for just a moment.
- Go ahead.

435
00:28:05,065 --> 00:28:07,065
[paper rustling]

436
00:28:12,385 --> 00:28:13,385
[Ruth] Alfonso.

437
00:28:13,465 --> 00:28:16,105
{\an8}CORONER ALFONSO GARCÍA MARÍN

438
00:28:24,385 --> 00:28:25,825
[music fades]

439
00:28:25,905 --> 00:28:28,185
[man] Our dear Lucía,
may God watch over you.

440
00:28:29,425 --> 00:28:31,265
You will be missed.

441
00:28:31,345 --> 00:28:33,625
Especially by your husband
and by your daughter.

442
00:28:34,665 --> 00:28:36,145
They are not alone.

443
00:28:36,225 --> 00:28:38,145
God smile upon this family

444
00:28:38,745 --> 00:28:40,265
until they are reunited.

445
00:28:41,225 --> 00:28:45,585
Faith is essential in times like these.

446
00:28:46,385 --> 00:28:50,305
It's so important to believe that
there is someone waiting for us.

447
00:28:50,385 --> 00:28:53,145
Back there, standing in the last row,
who is that woman?

448
00:28:54,785 --> 00:28:57,225
[man] It's easy to think of God
only in the hard times.

449
00:28:57,305 --> 00:28:58,385
No idea.

450
00:28:58,905 --> 00:29:00,545
But we must remember…

451
00:29:00,625 --> 00:29:03,345
I saw her back at the house.
At the wake too.

452
00:29:03,425 --> 00:29:06,145
- How orphaned are those without faith?
- Shh.

453
00:29:06,745 --> 00:29:08,945
Or those who worship false idols?

454
00:29:10,585 --> 00:29:13,185
Or those who are swept up by the absurd?

455
00:29:14,185 --> 00:29:17,225
Pursuing who knows what.

456
00:29:18,305 --> 00:29:21,305
Why do we have to sit here
and listen to this bullshit?

457
00:29:22,265 --> 00:29:25,945
[priest] To the false prophets,
to the merchants of the temple,

458
00:29:26,025 --> 00:29:28,625
to the ignorants
who claim enlightenment,

459
00:29:28,705 --> 00:29:30,905
to them, the Lord has this to say.

460
00:29:32,225 --> 00:29:35,865
"I am the way, and the truth,

461
00:29:36,385 --> 00:29:37,705
and the life."

462
00:29:37,785 --> 00:29:39,905
[people murmuring]

463
00:29:44,505 --> 00:29:45,705
[car horn honks]

464
00:29:51,625 --> 00:29:54,105
[dramatic music playing]

465
00:30:01,425 --> 00:30:03,745
[melancholy music playing]

466
00:30:22,545 --> 00:30:25,465
[church bell ringing]

467
00:30:31,065 --> 00:30:32,465
[music stops]

468
00:30:32,545 --> 00:30:33,825
Arón!

469
00:30:34,505 --> 00:30:35,505
Arón!

470
00:30:35,585 --> 00:30:37,025
[footsteps]

471
00:30:41,385 --> 00:30:42,385
Ruth.

472
00:30:44,105 --> 00:30:45,385
It's been a minute.

473
00:30:45,465 --> 00:30:46,465
Yeah.

474
00:30:52,545 --> 00:30:54,385
Hey, you dealing anymore?

475
00:30:54,465 --> 00:30:57,305
I'll find you something,
but only because it's you.

476
00:30:57,385 --> 00:30:58,905
Because I don't do this now.

477
00:30:58,985 --> 00:31:02,225
I'm moving up in the world.
I have a cleaning job now.

478
00:31:02,305 --> 00:31:03,665
[chuckles]

479
00:31:08,705 --> 00:31:09,705
For you.

480
00:31:10,745 --> 00:31:11,665
Thanks.

481
00:31:12,305 --> 00:31:14,745
I'm sorry I wasn't at the funeral.

482
00:31:15,545 --> 00:31:19,345
I thought your mom never really approved
of me, so it just didn't seem right.

483
00:31:19,865 --> 00:31:21,185
But I regret it now.

484
00:31:21,825 --> 00:31:25,585
You aren't going to funerals for the dead,
we go for the people left behind.

485
00:31:26,385 --> 00:31:29,265
And I was so grateful to everyone
who came to the funeral for my dad.

486
00:31:29,345 --> 00:31:30,825
Don't feel bad.

487
00:31:30,905 --> 00:31:33,385
You're right,
my mom wasn't your biggest fan.

488
00:31:33,465 --> 00:31:34,825
[chuckles]

489
00:31:35,425 --> 00:31:37,945
Well, I took the fall
for her daughter getting lost.

490
00:31:38,025 --> 00:31:39,265
What're you saying?

491
00:31:39,345 --> 00:31:40,745
I'm not lost, all right?

492
00:31:41,465 --> 00:31:43,905
I suppose it's true
that my life's not a straight line.

493
00:31:43,985 --> 00:31:45,185
Oh, how poetic.

494
00:31:46,145 --> 00:31:49,705
No, seriously. Seriously, I don't blame
her for having an issue with the boyfriend

495
00:31:49,785 --> 00:31:51,305
who introduced you to smoking.

496
00:31:51,385 --> 00:31:52,905
Hmm. Boyfriend?

497
00:31:52,985 --> 00:31:55,785
- Well, fuck buddy, okay? Fuck buddy.
- [laughs]

498
00:31:55,865 --> 00:31:57,745
She wasn't crazy about that either.

499
00:31:58,465 --> 00:31:59,545
[Ruth] Mm-mm.

500
00:32:01,145 --> 00:32:04,065
Lately, the one
that seems to hate me is your dad.

501
00:32:04,785 --> 00:32:05,825
How so?

502
00:32:05,905 --> 00:32:07,905
[quiet dramatic music playing]

503
00:32:07,985 --> 00:32:11,425
I don't know. He seemed less scary
when he was still in the Civil Guard.

504
00:32:11,505 --> 00:32:13,985
He used to offer me
these odd jobs every once in a while.

505
00:32:14,065 --> 00:32:16,545
Security jobs mostly,
A few hours and good pay.

506
00:32:17,425 --> 00:32:19,185
But since I took a job at Certyx…

507
00:32:20,785 --> 00:32:23,065
- Is that the new pharma company?
- Mm-hm.

508
00:32:23,705 --> 00:32:25,185
But he doesn't just ignore me,

509
00:32:25,265 --> 00:32:27,185
it's like he wants to see me dead.

510
00:32:32,185 --> 00:32:33,185
What?

511
00:32:35,465 --> 00:32:37,425
I don't know, I don't know…

512
00:32:37,505 --> 00:32:39,345
Maybe my father has a screw loose.

513
00:32:39,425 --> 00:32:42,185
But I just feel like
I'm the one who's going crazy here.

514
00:32:44,785 --> 00:32:46,465
He's been weird, hasn't he?

515
00:32:47,625 --> 00:32:48,825
Different, I guess.

516
00:32:50,145 --> 00:32:51,145
And?

517
00:32:53,505 --> 00:32:55,185
I've even been wondering if he…

518
00:32:58,945 --> 00:33:00,865
I'm not the same person I was either.

519
00:33:04,385 --> 00:33:05,425
You haven't changed.

520
00:33:09,305 --> 00:33:10,225
Hmm?

521
00:33:10,705 --> 00:33:11,745
Oh, shit.

522
00:33:14,065 --> 00:33:15,065
Was it?

523
00:33:17,625 --> 00:33:19,145
I survived colon cancer.

524
00:33:19,705 --> 00:33:20,825
You believe that?

525
00:33:23,785 --> 00:33:25,385
Jesus, I had no idea.

526
00:33:26,825 --> 00:33:29,865
Yeah, well, we hadn't talked in a bit.

527
00:33:29,945 --> 00:33:32,145
Not about to send an email to tell you.

528
00:33:32,225 --> 00:33:36,345
I suppose this bag has changed
my life in a few different ways, but…

529
00:33:36,425 --> 00:33:37,505
Well…

530
00:33:39,465 --> 00:33:42,025
Some days it's the worst thing
that's happened to me.

531
00:33:42,545 --> 00:33:44,105
And sometimes I'm thankful.

532
00:33:45,385 --> 00:33:47,625
It's proof I fought for my life and I won.

533
00:33:48,625 --> 00:33:50,865
That always makes me wanna celebrate.

534
00:33:53,305 --> 00:33:54,265
Oh, really?

535
00:33:54,345 --> 00:33:57,345
[lo-fi rock music playing]

536
00:33:57,425 --> 00:34:00,185
["His Eyes Keep Moving Towards Her"
by Los Cuantos]

537
00:34:04,545 --> 00:34:08,265
<i>♪ And he keeps his eyes to the ground ♪</i>

538
00:34:08,865 --> 00:34:13,025
<i>♪ Looking at his own feet</i>
<i>While walking around ♪</i>

539
00:34:13,105 --> 00:34:17,065
<i>♪ He knows every dog shit in town ♪</i>

540
00:34:17,625 --> 00:34:23,945
♪ <i>And he thinks he must be wrong</i>
<i>'Cause with his eyes closed… </i>♪

541
00:34:25,425 --> 00:34:26,425
What?

542
00:34:27,185 --> 00:34:28,265
It's just…

543
00:34:29,145 --> 00:34:30,985
Now, ever since I got the bag…

544
00:34:32,705 --> 00:34:34,265
What? Can't you do it?

545
00:34:34,345 --> 00:34:37,265
I don't know. Really, I have no idea.

546
00:34:38,785 --> 00:34:41,025
I haven't hooked up with anyone since.

547
00:34:41,105 --> 00:34:42,185
Why's that?

548
00:34:42,745 --> 00:34:43,865
It doesn't…

549
00:34:43,945 --> 00:34:46,105
make me feel very attractive, you know.

550
00:34:47,105 --> 00:34:48,145
I don't know.

551
00:34:48,905 --> 00:34:51,065
Well, I think you're hotter than ever.

552
00:34:51,145 --> 00:34:52,185
Really?

553
00:34:52,705 --> 00:34:53,985
Mmm.

554
00:34:54,065 --> 00:34:56,065
[song continues]

555
00:35:06,785 --> 00:35:08,225
[song fades]

556
00:35:09,785 --> 00:35:11,185
[Arón] Here's where I work.

557
00:35:12,625 --> 00:35:14,105
The new building's huge.

558
00:35:24,225 --> 00:35:26,145
That was a pretty great night.

559
00:35:26,905 --> 00:35:27,945
[Ruth] Mm.

560
00:35:29,225 --> 00:35:30,305
Mm-mm.

561
00:35:31,025 --> 00:35:31,985
See you around.

562
00:35:32,065 --> 00:35:33,225
See ya. You too.

563
00:35:35,465 --> 00:35:38,105
[melancholy piano music playing]

564
00:35:54,425 --> 00:35:56,425
[music fades]

565
00:35:56,505 --> 00:35:57,865
[birds chirping]

566
00:35:57,945 --> 00:35:59,945
[happy whistling]

567
00:36:00,025 --> 00:36:02,385
[scrubbing]

568
00:36:02,465 --> 00:36:03,985
[sighs]

569
00:36:10,465 --> 00:36:12,545
[whistling and scrubbing continue]

570
00:36:17,065 --> 00:36:18,465
[Ruth] What are you doing?

571
00:36:19,945 --> 00:36:21,105
What's it look like?

572
00:36:23,225 --> 00:36:24,545
Bit soon, don't you think?

573
00:36:25,465 --> 00:36:28,705
The sooner the better,
the sooner we can get on with our lives.

574
00:36:30,625 --> 00:36:33,105
And the sooner you
get back to the attic, right?

575
00:36:34,945 --> 00:36:35,985
[sighs]

576
00:36:42,425 --> 00:36:44,225
What is it you're doing up there?

577
00:36:47,185 --> 00:36:48,665
Hmm, well I…

578
00:36:48,745 --> 00:36:51,105
I read, watch videos, stay informed.

579
00:36:52,385 --> 00:36:54,305
That's what everyone should be doing.

580
00:36:56,025 --> 00:36:58,185
You need to keep
the door locked to do that?

581
00:36:58,265 --> 00:37:00,905
I don't like people sticking
their nose in my business.

582
00:37:00,985 --> 00:37:02,705
You were the same way.

583
00:37:02,785 --> 00:37:05,825
You made us put locks
on every door in the fucking house.

584
00:37:05,905 --> 00:37:07,425
The "fucking house"?

585
00:37:07,505 --> 00:37:08,825
Yeah.

586
00:37:08,905 --> 00:37:11,905
When I was a fucking teen, maybe.
Not like you.

587
00:37:12,505 --> 00:37:14,505
You never used to talk like that to me.

588
00:37:16,905 --> 00:37:19,585
Well, maybe that's
what you needed to hear, honey.

589
00:37:20,065 --> 00:37:21,385
A few cold, hard truths.

590
00:37:23,065 --> 00:37:24,305
I don't recognize you.

591
00:37:26,505 --> 00:37:28,865
[sighs] But you haven't changed at all.

592
00:37:29,505 --> 00:37:32,465
Your own mother's funeral
and you had somewhere else to be.

593
00:37:32,545 --> 00:37:35,265
[tense music playing]

594
00:37:35,345 --> 00:37:36,505
Gee, thanks.

595
00:37:56,145 --> 00:38:00,305
ADRENALINE - ADRENOCHROME

596
00:38:00,385 --> 00:38:02,385
[tense music continues]

597
00:38:17,105 --> 00:38:18,505
[tense music fades]

598
00:38:18,585 --> 00:38:20,305
Uh, Ruth. I…

599
00:38:20,385 --> 00:38:21,505
This is your mom's phone.

600
00:38:21,585 --> 00:38:23,625
I thought you could use it if you wanted.

601
00:38:24,305 --> 00:38:25,505
It's practically new.

602
00:38:25,585 --> 00:38:27,705
And I know yours is getting old.

603
00:38:29,745 --> 00:38:30,825
Thanks, Dad.

604
00:38:32,265 --> 00:38:35,025
- Whoa, this is one of the fancy ones.
- Yeah.

605
00:38:36,225 --> 00:38:39,065
She actually got
pretty annoyed with me about that.

606
00:38:39,145 --> 00:38:41,705
"It's too much to spend
on a phone," she said.

607
00:38:42,585 --> 00:38:44,025
So much bothered her.

608
00:38:44,865 --> 00:38:46,865
- Since when?
- [moody piano music playing]

609
00:38:46,945 --> 00:38:48,945
When it started, she was angry with you.

610
00:38:49,025 --> 00:38:51,945
Because you left,
because you had given up on your studies.

611
00:38:52,025 --> 00:38:54,385
You were God knows where
doing God knows what.

612
00:38:54,465 --> 00:38:57,905
But how could you be angry
with someone who wasn't there?

613
00:38:59,705 --> 00:39:02,185
I was the person who stayed here with her.

614
00:39:02,265 --> 00:39:05,105
The only person she could hold
responsible for it all.

615
00:39:05,625 --> 00:39:08,425
She blamed me and said
I wasn't strict enough with you.

616
00:39:09,505 --> 00:39:12,745
It's always easier to blame someone else.

617
00:39:16,105 --> 00:39:17,625
It was a difficult time.

618
00:39:19,225 --> 00:39:20,985
But don't worry about me.

619
00:39:21,065 --> 00:39:22,625
I'm doing much better now.

620
00:39:24,145 --> 00:39:25,305
I'm okay.

621
00:39:25,385 --> 00:39:27,545
[distant thunder rumbling]

622
00:39:27,625 --> 00:39:28,625
Just a minute.

623
00:39:29,785 --> 00:39:32,865
Uh, do you have
the password for Mom's phone?

624
00:39:32,945 --> 00:39:36,665
Uh… You won't need it.
I… I wiped it yesterday.

625
00:39:37,345 --> 00:39:38,225
Uh…

626
00:39:39,345 --> 00:39:41,065
And everything that was on it?

627
00:39:41,945 --> 00:39:43,745
Your mother's gone, sweetheart.

628
00:39:43,825 --> 00:39:46,265
Why would anyone else
need what was on it now?

629
00:39:46,905 --> 00:39:48,145
What use is it to you?

630
00:39:48,785 --> 00:39:51,345
- Me? Oh, nothing.
- [ominous music playing]

631
00:39:51,425 --> 00:39:53,505
- Never mind.
- All right.

632
00:39:53,585 --> 00:39:55,585
[intriguing music playing]

633
00:40:30,665 --> 00:40:31,665
[engine starts]

634
00:40:31,745 --> 00:40:33,945
["Amante Bandido"
by Miguel Bosé playing]

635
00:40:49,465 --> 00:40:50,705
[song stops]

636
00:40:54,905 --> 00:40:57,025
[tense music playing]

637
00:41:32,745 --> 00:41:36,025
[Ruth] He wiped her phone.
Anything we can do to see what was on it?

638
00:41:36,105 --> 00:41:38,945
One thing we can try.
We can see what she had in the cloud.

639
00:41:39,025 --> 00:41:41,105
But the repair shop
could probably do more.

640
00:41:41,185 --> 00:41:44,185
But we can't go into town,
everyone knows my father.

641
00:41:44,265 --> 00:41:45,625
Why does that matter?

642
00:41:48,425 --> 00:41:50,185
What the fuck's going on, Ruth?

643
00:41:53,665 --> 00:41:54,505
[phone chimes]

644
00:41:54,585 --> 00:41:56,865
[Lucía on recording]
<i>I thought your father was coming.</i>

645
00:41:56,945 --> 00:41:59,545
<i>He spends all day locked up in the attic.</i>

646
00:41:59,625 --> 00:42:03,345
<i>I'm afraid that, I don't, I don't know,</i>
<i>he'll do something crazy one day.</i>

647
00:42:03,425 --> 00:42:04,425
[phone chimes]

648
00:42:06,145 --> 00:42:09,305
All right. At your dad's age,
that could be anything, Ruth.

649
00:42:09,385 --> 00:42:10,385
Anything at all.

650
00:42:11,985 --> 00:42:15,825
- A divorce, or buying a convertible…
- Yeah, but she said she was scared, Arón.

651
00:42:16,545 --> 00:42:18,545
My mother was never afraid of anything.

652
00:42:19,425 --> 00:42:23,145
Yeah. You hadn't seen her in a while,
I'm just saying.

653
00:42:23,225 --> 00:42:24,825
For fuck's sake, she's my mom.

654
00:42:25,385 --> 00:42:27,545
She used to use the bathroom
with the door open.

655
00:42:28,225 --> 00:42:31,185
She couldn't stand closed doors
and she didn't like secrets.

656
00:42:31,705 --> 00:42:34,785
And I'm sure she hated that my dad
was locking himself upstairs.

657
00:42:36,945 --> 00:42:38,625
What if she went in there?

658
00:42:38,705 --> 00:42:39,785
What if she saw something?

659
00:42:39,865 --> 00:42:42,025
Whatever it is my father doesn't
want anybody to see?

660
00:42:42,105 --> 00:42:44,105
Maybe they fought
and that's why she called me?

661
00:42:45,065 --> 00:42:46,665
And that's why they killed her?

662
00:42:47,985 --> 00:42:50,625
Slow down, Ruth, come on.
Let's not get crazy.

663
00:42:51,185 --> 00:42:52,785
Have you talked to the Civil Guard?

664
00:42:52,865 --> 00:42:55,545
I can't do that,
they're all buddy-buddy with my father.

665
00:42:55,625 --> 00:42:56,825
I don't know.

666
00:42:56,905 --> 00:42:58,385
Am I crazy for thinking this?

667
00:42:58,465 --> 00:43:02,105
No, no, you're not. I just don't know.
I don't know, Ruth, I don't know.

668
00:43:04,905 --> 00:43:06,785
Is that why you're still in town?

669
00:43:11,745 --> 00:43:14,745
I need to know for sure
that what I'm thinking here isn't true.

670
00:43:15,865 --> 00:43:16,985
Here we go.

671
00:43:17,065 --> 00:43:18,385
What have we got?

672
00:43:18,465 --> 00:43:20,585
It says there are 200 photos.

673
00:43:21,345 --> 00:43:23,185
That doesn't seem like a lot.

674
00:43:23,265 --> 00:43:25,065
Yeah, this was a brand-new phone.

675
00:43:25,945 --> 00:43:27,825
I think it was a Christmas present.

676
00:43:28,585 --> 00:43:30,265
No, looks like that's it.

677
00:43:30,345 --> 00:43:32,785
Could there be another
folder here somewhere?

678
00:43:33,385 --> 00:43:35,705
Something she was trying
to keep hidden? I dunno.

679
00:43:35,785 --> 00:43:37,865
- Wait. What's that?
- Well, let's see.

680
00:43:38,345 --> 00:43:39,425
No name here.

681
00:43:40,185 --> 00:43:41,425
Close your eyes.

682
00:43:41,505 --> 00:43:42,465
Why?

683
00:43:42,545 --> 00:43:44,465
- Just in case.
- In case, what?

684
00:43:44,545 --> 00:43:46,625
What if there are nudes
or something like that?

685
00:43:46,705 --> 00:43:49,025
- Nudes of your mother, your father.
- Be serious.

686
00:43:49,105 --> 00:43:50,465
Or even both of them naked.

687
00:43:50,545 --> 00:43:52,625
- You don't want to see that.
- You're an idiot.

688
00:43:52,705 --> 00:43:55,425
Neither do I, for the record,
I swear, neither do I, but…

689
00:43:55,505 --> 00:43:58,225
But if that's what it's gonna
take to marry you, I'll do it.

690
00:43:58,905 --> 00:44:00,105
[scoffs]

691
00:44:00,185 --> 00:44:02,545
Relax, I'm kidding.
Come on, close your eyes.

692
00:44:04,225 --> 00:44:06,145
All right, hurry up. Go on.

693
00:44:07,625 --> 00:44:08,625
And?

694
00:44:09,545 --> 00:44:11,985
- Who is this?
- [mysterious music playing]

695
00:44:12,585 --> 00:44:13,625
[Ruth] Fuck!

696
00:44:15,185 --> 00:44:16,465
Fuck, I knew it!

697
00:44:16,985 --> 00:44:19,145
- I knew it.
- [Arón] What's going on?

698
00:44:19,225 --> 00:44:21,905
I saw this woman.
She was at the wake and the funeral.

699
00:44:21,985 --> 00:44:25,705
I asked my dad who she was
and he lied to me and said he had no idea.

700
00:44:26,185 --> 00:44:27,305
Who the fuck is she?

701
00:44:27,985 --> 00:44:31,385
I don't know, this doesn't give us a name.
Only your mom and dad know.

702
00:44:31,905 --> 00:44:34,385
I have to find out.
I need to figure out who she is.

703
00:44:34,465 --> 00:44:35,945
How do you plan to do that?

704
00:44:36,025 --> 00:44:39,825
I'll show all the photos to my father,
and I'll make him tell me the truth.

705
00:44:40,385 --> 00:44:41,825
Gonna do that on your own?

706
00:44:43,625 --> 00:44:45,625
[mysterious music continues]

707
00:44:51,185 --> 00:44:53,905
Shit, Arón, did you really
have to smoke in the van?

708
00:44:53,985 --> 00:44:55,385
I was nervous.

709
00:44:55,465 --> 00:44:56,625
But I'm good.

710
00:45:00,465 --> 00:45:01,465
[Ruth] Hi, Dad.

711
00:45:02,185 --> 00:45:03,385
Remember Arón?

712
00:45:08,465 --> 00:45:09,785
I invited him to dinner.

713
00:45:10,785 --> 00:45:11,625
Good evening.

714
00:45:12,265 --> 00:45:13,305
[snickers]

715
00:45:13,385 --> 00:45:14,585
That's perfect.

716
00:45:17,305 --> 00:45:19,945
Sorry. I haven't been
doing much cooking lately.

717
00:45:20,825 --> 00:45:22,745
I just throw things together.

718
00:45:22,865 --> 00:45:25,665
Don't worry, Martín,
this is Michelin-star cuisine for me.

719
00:45:25,745 --> 00:45:27,465
Do you eat a lot of junk food?

720
00:45:27,945 --> 00:45:30,825
I clean up shit for work in the morning
and I eat it at night.

721
00:45:30,905 --> 00:45:31,825
- Hm.
- Mm.

722
00:45:32,585 --> 00:45:34,465
Must be why you got cancer.

723
00:45:34,545 --> 00:45:35,625
Come on, Dad.

724
00:45:36,225 --> 00:45:38,665
No, I wasn't saying
that it's your fault, really.

725
00:45:38,745 --> 00:45:41,785
It's the chemicals they're putting
in everything nowadays.

726
00:45:42,665 --> 00:45:44,345
And it's no accident.

727
00:45:44,985 --> 00:45:47,745
You know, they make you sick
so that they can sell you the cure.

728
00:45:48,305 --> 00:45:51,065
At the same time, they're destroying
the National Health System

729
00:45:51,145 --> 00:45:52,705
so that we all have to pay.

730
00:45:54,985 --> 00:45:55,985
Who are "they"?

731
00:45:56,985 --> 00:46:00,665
- The people you've been working for.
- [chuckles] I clean the floors there.

732
00:46:02,025 --> 00:46:03,945
You're working for murderers, y'know.

733
00:46:04,025 --> 00:46:06,985
And they kidnap children too.
Can't forget that.

734
00:46:08,145 --> 00:46:09,625
Do you think this is funny?

735
00:46:09,705 --> 00:46:11,625
Wait, Martín, let me get this straight.

736
00:46:12,665 --> 00:46:15,545
You think my bosses
are responsible for my cancer?

737
00:46:15,625 --> 00:46:17,825
Maybe not your guys exactly, but…

738
00:46:18,745 --> 00:46:21,105
- Well, the pharma companies, you mean.
- Mm-hmm.

739
00:46:21,185 --> 00:46:22,665
And the governments.

740
00:46:22,745 --> 00:46:23,785
[laughs]

741
00:46:24,345 --> 00:46:25,585
Martín, come on now.

742
00:46:25,665 --> 00:46:27,625
I mean who else is to blame?

743
00:46:27,705 --> 00:46:30,865
Well, you know,
it's always easier to blame someone else.

744
00:46:30,945 --> 00:46:33,225
I wish someone was to blame.

745
00:46:33,305 --> 00:46:36,385
I wish there was a cancer lab somewhere,

746
00:46:36,465 --> 00:46:38,745
and we could all go down there
and destroy it.

747
00:46:38,825 --> 00:46:40,345
Just put an end to that shit.

748
00:46:40,865 --> 00:46:42,705
But that's not how it works, Martín.

749
00:46:42,785 --> 00:46:44,225
- It's not?
- No.

750
00:46:44,305 --> 00:46:45,465
How does it work, then?

751
00:46:45,545 --> 00:46:47,145
You get it, and you're fucked.

752
00:46:47,225 --> 00:46:49,425
And you don't know when it'll come back.

753
00:46:50,065 --> 00:46:51,985
You take that fear and you swallow it.

754
00:46:53,145 --> 00:46:55,105
But don't you smoke it
instead of swallowing it?

755
00:46:55,185 --> 00:46:56,305
[chuckles]

756
00:47:00,705 --> 00:47:04,025
Yeah, you smoke.
You sit around on the sofa doing nothing.

757
00:47:04,705 --> 00:47:06,945
You just allow them
to do whatever they want.

758
00:47:07,025 --> 00:47:08,905
And what do you do, remind me?

759
00:47:08,985 --> 00:47:13,305
I do my research,
investigate, ask questions, yeah?

760
00:47:13,905 --> 00:47:14,905
Hmm.

761
00:47:15,665 --> 00:47:17,545
That what you're doing
with that friend of yours,

762
00:47:17,625 --> 00:47:20,625
the woman who was at the wake,
the one you said you didn't know?

763
00:47:22,105 --> 00:47:25,425
Dad, Mom had photos of you
and this woman together on her phone.

764
00:47:26,465 --> 00:47:27,825
Walking around town.

765
00:47:28,665 --> 00:47:29,905
What are you doing, honey?

766
00:47:29,985 --> 00:47:31,905
Uh… investigating, like you.

767
00:47:33,145 --> 00:47:34,145
Who is she?

768
00:47:36,145 --> 00:47:37,585
Her name is Berta.

769
00:47:39,305 --> 00:47:40,385
Her name is Berta.

770
00:47:40,865 --> 00:47:41,825
Perfect.

771
00:47:43,785 --> 00:47:44,665
Who is Berta?

772
00:47:45,265 --> 00:47:46,585
Berta is a friend.

773
00:47:47,105 --> 00:47:47,945
A friend.

774
00:47:48,025 --> 00:47:50,625
Believe it or not, at my age,
you can make new friends.

775
00:47:50,705 --> 00:47:51,585
[Ruth] Hmm.

776
00:47:51,665 --> 00:47:55,305
But people in town could easily get
the wrong idea about something like this,

777
00:47:55,385 --> 00:47:57,305
so I prefer not to say anything.

778
00:47:57,985 --> 00:47:59,745
- But where?
- What?

779
00:47:59,825 --> 00:48:01,585
- Where?
- Where did you meet her?

780
00:48:03,145 --> 00:48:05,265
- On the internet.
- [Ruth] On Tinder?

781
00:48:06,705 --> 00:48:09,665
No. We met on a forum for research.

782
00:48:09,745 --> 00:48:11,145
Who goes on those websites?

783
00:48:11,225 --> 00:48:13,305
More people than you might think.

784
00:48:13,985 --> 00:48:15,185
- Really?
- Yeah.

785
00:48:15,945 --> 00:48:18,385
Most people on there are discreet because…

786
00:48:19,345 --> 00:48:20,425
Because…

787
00:48:21,465 --> 00:48:24,145
Because it's not easy
to talk about certain things.

788
00:48:25,545 --> 00:48:28,025
Because along the way you can lose people.

789
00:48:29,825 --> 00:48:31,545
Is that what happened to you, Dad?

790
00:48:33,025 --> 00:48:34,225
With Mom?

791
00:48:36,425 --> 00:48:37,345
Maybe, yeah.

792
00:48:40,865 --> 00:48:42,705
Berta has lost a lot of people.

793
00:48:43,425 --> 00:48:46,785
She lost her husband,
she lost custody of their two children.

794
00:48:47,705 --> 00:48:49,665
And right now she's feeling very alone.

795
00:48:49,745 --> 00:48:53,465
As her friend, I've been trying to keep
her company. I spend time with her.

796
00:48:53,545 --> 00:48:55,865
I'm helping out a friend, you know.

797
00:48:55,945 --> 00:48:57,225
What's wrong with that?

798
00:49:11,505 --> 00:49:12,865
Tell me what you think.

799
00:49:14,105 --> 00:49:15,465
I understand how you feel.

800
00:49:15,545 --> 00:49:16,545
I do.

801
00:49:16,625 --> 00:49:19,185
But deep down,
underneath everything that's…

802
00:49:19,265 --> 00:49:22,665
that's going on with him,
at the end of the day, he's still himself.

803
00:49:23,425 --> 00:49:26,705
He's a Civil Guard,
but he's not a bad guy.

804
00:49:26,785 --> 00:49:29,905
Even in his little fantasy,
he still sees himself as the hero,

805
00:49:29,985 --> 00:49:31,865
saving children from the evil elite.

806
00:49:37,665 --> 00:49:38,785
[chuckles faintly]

807
00:49:39,665 --> 00:49:40,625
Hey.

808
00:49:41,225 --> 00:49:43,905
- Your room brings back some memories.
- [sighs]

809
00:49:44,545 --> 00:49:46,585
I'm getting turned on
thinking about it now.

810
00:49:46,665 --> 00:49:48,945
Your dad next door,
just like it was back then.

811
00:49:49,025 --> 00:49:50,105
Ugh, Arón.

812
00:49:56,225 --> 00:49:57,185
[Ruth sighs]

813
00:49:57,905 --> 00:49:59,705
Do you know how to pick a lock?

814
00:50:02,025 --> 00:50:05,265
I need to get into the attic and
see what my mother saw in there.

815
00:50:06,545 --> 00:50:08,625
[nervously] Why are you telling me this?

816
00:50:09,745 --> 00:50:13,465
It's just I don't think I know anybody
else here who knows how to pick a lock.

817
00:50:14,505 --> 00:50:15,585
And I do?

818
00:50:16,225 --> 00:50:18,345
Why? Because I sold weed?

819
00:50:18,945 --> 00:50:21,785
No. It's just that I was hoping
you could help me.

820
00:50:21,865 --> 00:50:22,865
[Arón chuckles]

821
00:50:23,745 --> 00:50:25,305
Ruth, I'm not a criminal.

822
00:50:26,785 --> 00:50:27,865
[Ruth chuckles]

823
00:50:29,505 --> 00:50:30,905
I know that, Arón.

824
00:50:30,985 --> 00:50:34,465
I don't know, I mean I thought it was
possible you might know a guy, or…

825
00:50:34,545 --> 00:50:38,145
Who do you think I hang around with?
Who do you think I am?

826
00:50:38,225 --> 00:50:40,625
[gloomy music playing]

827
00:50:41,585 --> 00:50:43,545
It doesn't matter. Forget it.

828
00:50:44,425 --> 00:50:45,425
[door opens]

829
00:51:19,585 --> 00:51:23,545
[man on video] <i>To start, we're going</i>
<i>to take the cylinder out very carefully.</i>

830
00:51:23,625 --> 00:51:26,905
<i>You're gonna want to watch</i>
<i>the terminal blocks here and make sure…</i>

831
00:51:43,105 --> 00:51:44,265
[clicks]

832
00:51:44,345 --> 00:51:46,345
[tense music playing]

833
00:51:46,865 --> 00:51:48,545
[shudders]

834
00:51:52,945 --> 00:51:55,945
[cell phone buzzing]

835
00:52:04,385 --> 00:52:06,385
[music swelling]

836
00:52:12,585 --> 00:52:13,625
[clicks tongue]

837
00:52:17,345 --> 00:52:18,505
[door opening]

838
00:52:24,705 --> 00:52:26,705
[tense music continues]

839
00:52:27,905 --> 00:52:29,105
[door shuts]

840
00:52:45,305 --> 00:52:47,305
[music becomes quiet]

841
00:53:03,665 --> 00:53:05,545
- [door opens]
- [music fades]

842
00:53:05,625 --> 00:53:07,145
[door creaks]

843
00:53:08,225 --> 00:53:09,225
[door closes]

844
00:53:10,665 --> 00:53:11,705
[lock clicks]

845
00:53:14,665 --> 00:53:15,985
[line ringing]

846
00:53:16,825 --> 00:53:17,825
[Arón] <i>Yeah?</i>

847
00:53:17,905 --> 00:53:20,745
I uh… Sorry, Arón, I, uh…

848
00:53:20,825 --> 00:53:22,505
<i>Did I leave my pass somewhere?</i>

849
00:53:23,385 --> 00:53:24,225
Your what?

850
00:53:24,305 --> 00:53:27,625
<i>My key card,</i>
<i>with the photo and the barcode.</i>

851
00:53:27,705 --> 00:53:30,385
- Um…
<i>- It's on a blue lanyard. I can't find it.</i>

852
00:53:30,465 --> 00:53:33,465
- Where did you leave it?
<i>- Hanging in the van. I can't find it.</i>

853
00:53:33,545 --> 00:53:35,985
<i>I thought I might have left it</i>
<i>at your place yesterday.</i>

854
00:53:36,065 --> 00:53:39,025
Can anyone get into the pharmaceutical
company if they have that pass?

855
00:53:39,105 --> 00:53:40,345
<i>Yes, of course.</i>

856
00:53:40,425 --> 00:53:41,745
[mysterious music playing]

857
00:53:41,825 --> 00:53:43,785
- Okay…
<i>- Ruth, what are you thinking?</i>

858
00:53:44,425 --> 00:53:46,425
- It's weird, isn't it?
<i>- What do you mean?</i>

859
00:53:46,505 --> 00:53:48,705
Don't you think it's strange,
that you come here

860
00:53:48,785 --> 00:53:50,745
and suddenly
your pass goes missing?

861
00:53:50,825 --> 00:53:53,105
- Leave the van unlocked?
<i>- I'm sure I left it somewhere.</i>

862
00:53:53,185 --> 00:53:56,385
<i>Just wanted to check if it was there.</i>
<i>I'll get a new one. Bye.</i>

863
00:53:56,465 --> 00:53:59,425
- [disconnect tone]
- [moody dramatic music playing]

864
00:53:59,505 --> 00:54:01,665
- [phone clicks]
- [sighs]

865
00:54:08,985 --> 00:54:11,625
WHAT THEY DON'T WANT US TO KNOW

866
00:54:13,905 --> 00:54:16,945
LAS VEGAS PUBLIC SCHOOL

867
00:54:23,865 --> 00:54:25,425
My ex-wife isn't well.

868
00:54:27,785 --> 00:54:30,065
I asked a judge for a restraining order.

869
00:54:30,145 --> 00:54:31,305
And they granted it.

870
00:54:32,145 --> 00:54:33,665
Right away.

871
00:54:33,745 --> 00:54:37,265
They could clearly see
that she's dangerous.

872
00:54:38,105 --> 00:54:39,305
But dangerous how?

873
00:54:39,785 --> 00:54:41,305
She lost her mind.

874
00:54:41,385 --> 00:54:42,545
She was…

875
00:54:42,625 --> 00:54:43,665
[exhales]

876
00:54:43,745 --> 00:54:46,705
She was a wonderful woman,
she was just amazing.

877
00:54:47,905 --> 00:54:49,945
And then she snapped.

878
00:54:50,865 --> 00:54:52,905
She was sick. She was depressed.

879
00:54:52,985 --> 00:54:55,385
It lasted many months.

880
00:54:56,065 --> 00:54:58,265
It was awful, just terrible.

881
00:54:59,385 --> 00:55:02,385
And she finally just shut herself away.

882
00:55:02,465 --> 00:55:04,625
She became obsessed
with these absurd ideas.

883
00:55:04,705 --> 00:55:08,385
She said that birds weren't real,
that they were watching us.

884
00:55:09,065 --> 00:55:12,465
There were these planes that were
fumigating nanotechnology particles

885
00:55:12,545 --> 00:55:13,905
to control us.

886
00:55:13,985 --> 00:55:17,545
Suddenly, there were evil people
who controlled everything, and she…

887
00:55:17,625 --> 00:55:19,385
It was all up to her to defeat them,

888
00:55:19,465 --> 00:55:22,065
and somehow everything
in the world would be right.

889
00:55:23,185 --> 00:55:26,025
Next, she became obsessed
with the disappearances.

890
00:55:26,865 --> 00:55:28,585
With the missing children.

891
00:55:28,665 --> 00:55:31,625
You weren't able to convince her
that it was nonsense, these beliefs?

892
00:55:31,705 --> 00:55:32,785
I wish.

893
00:55:32,865 --> 00:55:36,145
But then she started
overprotecting my children, <i>our </i>children.

894
00:55:36,745 --> 00:55:38,985
They'd come home
and she would search their clothes,

895
00:55:39,065 --> 00:55:41,025
looking in their backpacks for cameras.

896
00:55:41,105 --> 00:55:42,305
They couldn't breathe.

897
00:55:42,385 --> 00:55:44,865
She was so… controlling.

898
00:55:46,945 --> 00:55:49,865
Then she renewed her gun license
and had them in the house.

899
00:55:50,545 --> 00:55:53,865
It didn't stop,
things just kept getting worse and worse.

900
00:55:54,465 --> 00:55:58,025
My ex-wife was an explosives expert
when she was in the army.

901
00:55:59,625 --> 00:56:01,985
It seemed like she was gonna
blow something up.

902
00:56:02,065 --> 00:56:03,465
Something, somewhere…

903
00:56:03,945 --> 00:56:05,265
I'm sure that…

904
00:56:06,745 --> 00:56:08,745
I'll see her name in the papers someday.

905
00:56:09,305 --> 00:56:10,385
I know it.

906
00:56:11,985 --> 00:56:14,265
My father is like
a totally different person.

907
00:56:15,065 --> 00:56:16,865
I know it's hard to hear but…

908
00:56:17,945 --> 00:56:19,145
but get away from him.

909
00:56:19,785 --> 00:56:22,385
Run away. As far as you can.

910
00:56:32,625 --> 00:56:33,625
[gunshot]

911
00:56:34,505 --> 00:56:36,505
[distant dogs barking]

912
00:56:49,025 --> 00:56:50,025
[gun shot]

913
00:57:00,265 --> 00:57:01,905
- [gun shot]
- [pottery breaking]

914
00:57:03,945 --> 00:57:05,105
What are you doing?

915
00:57:06,825 --> 00:57:07,865
Practicing.

916
00:57:08,665 --> 00:57:09,665
Something wrong?

917
00:57:12,625 --> 00:57:14,425
I've never seen you shoot before.

918
00:57:15,025 --> 00:57:16,105
[scoffs]

919
00:57:22,825 --> 00:57:25,665
They didn't make you give back
your gun when you retired?

920
00:57:27,065 --> 00:57:29,505
You never know when
it might be good to have around.

921
00:57:36,105 --> 00:57:38,785
[suspenseful music playing]

922
00:57:44,945 --> 00:57:46,625
- [gun shot]
- [pottery breaking]

923
00:58:09,745 --> 00:58:11,505
[faint creaking]

924
00:58:13,265 --> 00:58:14,265
[music fades]

925
00:58:14,345 --> 00:58:16,345
[footsteps approaching]

926
00:58:18,505 --> 00:58:20,785
[floor boards creaking]

927
00:58:28,945 --> 00:58:30,945
[floorboards creaking]

928
00:58:42,145 --> 00:58:43,905
[paper rustling]

929
00:59:04,585 --> 00:59:07,105
[tense music playing]

930
00:59:34,065 --> 00:59:35,385
Good night, Ruth.

931
00:59:44,225 --> 00:59:45,505
[footsteps receding]

932
00:59:45,585 --> 00:59:47,305
[music stops]

933
00:59:49,025 --> 00:59:50,105
[exhales]

934
00:59:50,745 --> 00:59:52,785
[Ruth] I need a few more days, I can't…

935
00:59:53,665 --> 00:59:56,905
My mom just died.
I'm… I'm helping my dad here, and…

936
00:59:57,945 --> 01:00:00,265
Okay, fine, fire me. Like… [scoffs]

937
01:00:00,345 --> 01:00:02,425
You're not the only shitty club in Berlin.

938
01:00:02,505 --> 01:00:03,505
Yes.

939
01:00:05,385 --> 01:00:06,225
[phone clicks]

940
01:00:10,345 --> 01:00:12,745
Who was that on the phone?
Is something wrong?

941
01:00:12,825 --> 01:00:15,225
No. It was just my job in Berlin.

942
01:00:15,785 --> 01:00:16,785
Uh-huh.

943
01:00:20,345 --> 01:00:21,625
Where are you going?

944
01:00:22,145 --> 01:00:24,585
We're still out looking for Alejandro.

945
01:00:25,145 --> 01:00:27,505
Uh… I'm headed to the lake.

946
01:00:27,585 --> 01:00:30,585
You wanna come?
You loved it when you were little.

947
01:00:32,185 --> 01:00:33,185
Um…

948
01:00:36,105 --> 01:00:38,225
Well, mm, okay.

949
01:00:38,305 --> 01:00:40,305
[intriguing music playing]

950
01:00:44,585 --> 01:00:48,545
[Martín] We've combed over each inch
of the forest and we've found nothing.

951
01:00:48,625 --> 01:00:52,745
If he had gotten lost,
or fallen, or he had died somehow,

952
01:00:52,825 --> 01:00:55,225
well, then we would have
found his body already.

953
01:00:55,305 --> 01:00:56,785
[music stops]

954
01:00:56,865 --> 01:00:58,665
So, what are we doing here?

955
01:00:58,745 --> 01:01:01,065
- There are these old tunnels…
- Old tunnels?

956
01:01:01,145 --> 01:01:03,465
Yeah. They're hundreds of years old.

957
01:01:03,545 --> 01:01:08,065
During the war, the Maquis used them
to secretly go see their families in town.

958
01:01:08,145 --> 01:01:09,465
Here.

959
01:01:11,145 --> 01:01:12,145
Look at this.

960
01:01:13,025 --> 01:01:15,425
Do you know what's there now,
on the other end?

961
01:01:15,505 --> 01:01:17,385
- What?
- The pharma company.

962
01:01:17,905 --> 01:01:19,905
[melancholy music playing]

963
01:01:21,425 --> 01:01:22,705
Right.

964
01:01:22,785 --> 01:01:23,945
Let's go.

965
01:01:34,625 --> 01:01:36,865
NO SWIMMING

966
01:01:36,945 --> 01:01:37,985
[Ruth sighs]

967
01:01:51,305 --> 01:01:52,945
Is that enough for today, maybe?

968
01:01:54,625 --> 01:01:57,625
What if the entrance to the tunnels
was somewhere in the lake?

969
01:01:58,745 --> 01:02:00,945
They said there were spills
a few years back.

970
01:02:01,025 --> 01:02:03,145
That's why nobody swims here anymore.

971
01:02:03,945 --> 01:02:07,465
People use it as a dump,
but what if it's just a cover-up

972
01:02:07,545 --> 01:02:10,705
so that no one actually goes in the water
and finds the tunnels?

973
01:02:13,265 --> 01:02:15,545
I'll have to come back with a wetsuit.

974
01:02:15,625 --> 01:02:17,065
You know what?

975
01:02:17,145 --> 01:02:18,665
That won't be necessary.

976
01:02:20,625 --> 01:02:21,785
What're you doing?

977
01:02:22,385 --> 01:02:24,825
- Finding those tunnels.
- What are you talking about?

978
01:02:25,345 --> 01:02:27,865
Come on,
you can't go in there, it's filthy!

979
01:02:30,025 --> 01:02:31,945
Where are the tunnels, Dad?

980
01:02:32,025 --> 01:02:34,185
This way? Or over here?

981
01:02:36,905 --> 01:02:38,665
What do you think you're doing?

982
01:02:38,745 --> 01:02:40,465
The same thing as you!

983
01:02:40,545 --> 01:02:42,865
The same insane shit
you pull every single day!

984
01:02:42,945 --> 01:02:44,825
It's not so funny, is it?

985
01:02:44,905 --> 01:02:47,345
[melancholy music continues]

986
01:02:54,025 --> 01:02:55,505
Well, I don't see anything.

987
01:02:57,545 --> 01:02:59,145
Or maybe over here?

988
01:02:59,225 --> 01:03:00,225
Ah!

989
01:03:02,025 --> 01:03:03,145
[Martín] What's wrong?

990
01:03:05,825 --> 01:03:06,825
[groans]

991
01:03:10,265 --> 01:03:12,425
- I cut myself.
- Well, I told you.

992
01:03:19,505 --> 01:03:21,585
[thunder rumbling]

993
01:03:23,985 --> 01:03:25,265
[Ruth inhales sharply]

994
01:03:29,185 --> 01:03:30,505
Time to go home, yeah?

995
01:03:31,105 --> 01:03:32,145
What?

996
01:03:32,225 --> 01:03:34,465
There's all kinds
of nasty crap in the water.

997
01:03:34,545 --> 01:03:37,425
I know. In fact, I warned you.

998
01:03:37,505 --> 01:03:39,665
We have to clean this
so it doesn't get infected.

999
01:03:39,745 --> 01:03:40,985
Just a scrape.

1000
01:03:41,065 --> 01:03:44,465
I wanna check something out up there.
Hurry up, it's gonna rain.

1001
01:03:47,105 --> 01:03:48,145
[sighs]

1002
01:03:57,425 --> 01:04:01,745
Seems like the only thing you care about
is your obsession with finding this kid.

1003
01:04:01,825 --> 01:04:05,145
Mom's barely in the ground and you're back
out here searching already.

1004
01:04:05,225 --> 01:04:08,305
I don't know, it seems like
you're pissed off about Mom

1005
01:04:08,385 --> 01:04:10,905
only because it interrupted
your search for two days.

1006
01:04:10,985 --> 01:04:13,825
Are you telling me that
my wife's death doesn't matter to me?

1007
01:04:13,905 --> 01:04:16,545
How come you care
so much about this kid, Dad?

1008
01:04:17,425 --> 01:04:19,145
Are you trying to impress her?

1009
01:04:19,225 --> 01:04:20,225
Who do you mean?

1010
01:04:21,345 --> 01:04:22,705
Your new friend, Berta.

1011
01:04:22,785 --> 01:04:24,825
Did she brainwash you, huh, Dad?

1012
01:04:25,745 --> 01:04:28,385
I don't know, tell me.
Are you in love or something?

1013
01:04:28,905 --> 01:04:31,225
The only woman I've ever loved
was your mother.

1014
01:04:31,305 --> 01:04:32,825
- Hm.
- In spite of the drinking.

1015
01:04:33,465 --> 01:04:37,425
In spite of her suddenly being so devout
she would never miss a Sunday mass.

1016
01:04:37,505 --> 01:04:39,865
Your mom hadn't been well
for a long time, Ruth.

1017
01:04:40,385 --> 01:04:41,625
Not since you left.

1018
01:04:42,985 --> 01:04:44,705
Oh, so now it's all my fault?

1019
01:04:45,425 --> 01:04:47,145
You wanna know why I left home?

1020
01:04:47,225 --> 01:04:50,705
Because I wanted to quit swimming.
But of course you'd never allow that.

1021
01:04:50,785 --> 01:04:53,465
I wasn't happy, Dad. I wanted my own life.

1022
01:04:54,905 --> 01:04:56,865
Your mother adored you, Ruth.

1023
01:04:57,825 --> 01:05:01,465
You were the light of her life.
When you left, you left her in the dark.

1024
01:05:01,545 --> 01:05:03,905
Does it surprise you
that she'd been drinking?

1025
01:05:06,185 --> 01:05:09,865
Maybe she started drinking because she
couldn't stand being with an angry old man

1026
01:05:09,945 --> 01:05:13,625
who was getting crazier every day
with his conspiracy theory bullshit.

1027
01:05:13,705 --> 01:05:17,105
Do you know how hard it is to love
someone who looks at you like that?

1028
01:05:17,185 --> 01:05:19,905
After their love and respect
for you goes away?

1029
01:05:19,985 --> 01:05:22,505
Who only sees you
as a useless old man all of a sudden?

1030
01:05:26,265 --> 01:05:28,265
[thunder rumbling]

1031
01:05:31,025 --> 01:05:34,545
I guess it's my fault that you've become
so sick in the head now, too, right?

1032
01:05:34,625 --> 01:05:36,265
I'm completely sane.

1033
01:05:36,345 --> 01:05:38,705
More sane than ever.
Because now I'm awake.

1034
01:05:38,785 --> 01:05:42,465
It's you and the rest of them
who still prefer living in ignorance,

1035
01:05:42,545 --> 01:05:45,105
choosing to ignore the pain
of those children, their families.

1036
01:05:45,185 --> 01:05:49,225
You gotta be kidding, stop. I can't listen
to you spouting the same bullshit!

1037
01:05:49,305 --> 01:05:50,305
I'm not crazy!

1038
01:05:50,905 --> 01:05:53,025
If there was an epidemic
of missing children,

1039
01:05:53,105 --> 01:05:55,745
it would be all over the front page
of every newspaper.

1040
01:05:55,825 --> 01:05:57,625
You're so naive, honey.

1041
01:05:57,705 --> 01:06:01,025
Do I really have to explain
to you how the world works?

1042
01:06:01,105 --> 01:06:02,425
[thunder rumbling]

1043
01:06:02,505 --> 01:06:06,585
I'd prefer to think that you are naive,
and not something worse.

1044
01:06:06,665 --> 01:06:08,345
[dramatic music playing]

1045
01:06:08,425 --> 01:06:10,585
Okay, great, so now
you're suspicious of me?

1046
01:06:11,625 --> 01:06:14,585
Of course, Dad, everyone here
except for you has been bribed.

1047
01:06:14,665 --> 01:06:16,265
Everyone's an idiot except you.

1048
01:06:16,345 --> 01:06:18,265
Have you looked in the mirror lately?

1049
01:06:18,745 --> 01:06:20,465
Have you seen what you look like?

1050
01:06:20,545 --> 01:06:23,505
Because you don't exactly look
like a genius to me right now.

1051
01:06:23,585 --> 01:06:27,545
You're not this prophet everyone should
follow because only you know the truth.

1052
01:06:28,305 --> 01:06:31,745
You're just an old man locked in an attic
doing God knows what up there.

1053
01:06:31,825 --> 01:06:33,545
That's the kind of guy you've become.

1054
01:06:34,545 --> 01:06:37,265
You want to know
what I do in the attic, is that it?

1055
01:06:38,025 --> 01:06:40,745
Is that why you tried
to break in yesterday, huh?

1056
01:06:43,145 --> 01:06:44,945
So you've been spying on me, then?

1057
01:06:45,665 --> 01:06:47,265
- Perfect.
- Where are you going?

1058
01:06:47,345 --> 01:06:49,905
- Get your fucking hands off!
- You'll get lost! Ruth!

1059
01:06:52,225 --> 01:06:54,185
- Ruth. Ruth!
- Leave me alone!

1060
01:06:55,865 --> 01:06:56,705
Ruth.

1061
01:06:57,705 --> 01:06:58,665
Ruth!

1062
01:06:59,305 --> 01:07:00,145
Come back!

1063
01:07:00,745 --> 01:07:01,825
Leave me alone!

1064
01:07:08,665 --> 01:07:10,065
- [grunts]
- Ruth!

1065
01:07:25,105 --> 01:07:27,185
[dramatic music fades]

1066
01:07:27,265 --> 01:07:30,185
[man] You lost consciousness,
but you're looking okay now.

1067
01:07:30,265 --> 01:07:34,145
You'll need to take it easy for at least
a couple of days, lay off that ankle.

1068
01:07:34,945 --> 01:07:37,785
Take one painkiller
every eight hours if it hurts.

1069
01:07:37,865 --> 01:07:39,865
And your dad should make tea for you.

1070
01:07:39,945 --> 01:07:41,265
He's good with herbs.

1071
01:07:41,345 --> 01:07:42,945
But, above all, rest.

1072
01:07:43,025 --> 01:07:45,465
We want that ankle
to heal properly, all right?

1073
01:07:48,145 --> 01:07:49,145
Yes, okay.

1074
01:07:51,185 --> 01:07:53,185
[somber music playing]

1075
01:07:54,465 --> 01:07:56,025
[Ruth inhales sharply]

1076
01:08:12,385 --> 01:08:13,865
[car chirps]

1077
01:09:03,145 --> 01:09:05,385
[unsettling music playing]

1078
01:09:27,105 --> 01:09:28,465
[music fades]

1079
01:09:28,545 --> 01:09:31,105
Why the fuck is the house
filled with cameras, Dad?

1080
01:09:32,105 --> 01:09:34,465
Are you spying on me
or were you spying on Mom?

1081
01:09:34,945 --> 01:09:38,305
[sighs] Why would I do that?
Stop acting like a child, will ya?

1082
01:09:38,385 --> 01:09:40,105
I'm the one acting like a child?

1083
01:09:40,185 --> 01:09:42,185
I put them up for security.

1084
01:09:42,265 --> 01:09:45,505
Because I was afraid that
those people would be coming after us.

1085
01:09:45,585 --> 01:09:46,625
For God's sake…

1086
01:09:46,705 --> 01:09:49,145
- See? I knew you wouldn't believe me.
- [scoffs]

1087
01:09:49,225 --> 01:09:50,705
That's why I didn't tell you.

1088
01:09:50,785 --> 01:09:53,865
But who exactly
is gonna come after us, Dad? Who?

1089
01:09:53,945 --> 01:09:56,545
- The pharmaceutical company?
- And the politicians.

1090
01:09:56,625 --> 01:09:59,505
- Oh.
- And the police. They can bribe anyone.

1091
01:09:59,585 --> 01:10:01,265
The ones you least suspect.

1092
01:10:02,225 --> 01:10:03,665
Just think about it.

1093
01:10:04,225 --> 01:10:06,625
They're committing atrocities on kids.

1094
01:10:07,105 --> 01:10:10,625
They're capable of doing anything
to people who oppose their mission.

1095
01:10:11,145 --> 01:10:12,585
And they know I'm onto them.

1096
01:10:13,105 --> 01:10:15,945
I've had to be extra careful
since they caught me looking around.

1097
01:10:16,025 --> 01:10:20,865
A guard asked me for my ID. I refused,
but they know who I am and where I live.

1098
01:10:24,705 --> 01:10:26,665
Why were you there in the first place?

1099
01:10:27,225 --> 01:10:29,905
The tunnels connect there,
to the basement of the Certyx building.

1100
01:10:29,985 --> 01:10:31,825
Come on, stop it. I've had enough.

1101
01:10:32,465 --> 01:10:34,825
I'm not gonna sit with a poker face
while you go on and on

1102
01:10:34,905 --> 01:10:36,625
about abandoned tunnels, kids taken.

1103
01:10:36,705 --> 01:10:39,385
- I can't do it. I don't understand you.
- You're not listening.

1104
01:10:39,465 --> 01:10:40,665
They're very dangerous.

1105
01:10:41,265 --> 01:10:44,225
The only one following me around
is your friend Berta.

1106
01:10:44,305 --> 01:10:46,225
She's protecting you, Ruth.

1107
01:10:47,785 --> 01:10:48,985
Protecting?

1108
01:10:49,065 --> 01:10:51,625
She's just been trying
to make sure you're all right.

1109
01:10:51,705 --> 01:10:54,785
And because you're my daughter,
that makes you a target, Ruth.

1110
01:11:01,185 --> 01:11:04,385
Were the cameras recording everything
on the night that Mom died?

1111
01:11:05,665 --> 01:11:08,065
- Of course.
- [intriguing music playing]

1112
01:11:08,145 --> 01:11:09,225
What do they show?

1113
01:11:09,305 --> 01:11:12,425
They show your mom
going into the bathroom.

1114
01:11:12,505 --> 01:11:13,545
That's all.

1115
01:11:15,145 --> 01:11:16,665
Do the Civil Guard know about it?

1116
01:11:16,745 --> 01:11:20,465
Yeah. They have all the footage.
That was the first thing I did, obviously.

1117
01:11:21,585 --> 01:11:23,425
Why didn't you tell me anything? Hm?

1118
01:11:23,505 --> 01:11:26,025
Because you wouldn't
have believed anything I said.

1119
01:11:28,105 --> 01:11:29,745
[intriguing music fades]

1120
01:11:31,945 --> 01:11:33,465
- [Ruth] Thank you.
- Of course.

1121
01:11:34,745 --> 01:11:35,825
I have it here.

1122
01:11:36,985 --> 01:11:39,665
This video shows your mom
in the bathroom already.

1123
01:11:40,225 --> 01:11:41,385
This was the day…

1124
01:11:42,305 --> 01:11:44,105
- That she died?
- Yes, that's right.

1125
01:11:54,505 --> 01:11:56,385
- Is it muted?
- [officer] There's no audio.

1126
01:11:56,465 --> 01:11:59,305
So we don't know if she said
something through the door.

1127
01:12:00,145 --> 01:12:02,305
What if my father's just
trying to put on a show?

1128
01:12:02,385 --> 01:12:05,745
Well, that would mean that your father's
planned around the cameras,

1129
01:12:05,825 --> 01:12:10,345
in order to give himself an alibi, so he
could then kill your mother in cold blood.

1130
01:12:11,105 --> 01:12:12,385
Is that what you think?

1131
01:12:13,305 --> 01:12:17,145
No, but, my mom locks the door,
and that's it?

1132
01:12:17,745 --> 01:12:19,545
- Why does she fall?
- The alcohol.

1133
01:12:20,345 --> 01:12:22,985
Yeah, but my mom
couldn't have been drinking that much.

1134
01:12:23,785 --> 01:12:24,705
Okay.

1135
01:12:26,545 --> 01:12:28,985
This footage was recorded
a few days earlier.

1136
01:12:29,065 --> 01:12:30,705
It might be hard to watch.

1137
01:12:35,265 --> 01:12:38,185
She'd obviously also
been drinking that day.

1138
01:12:38,265 --> 01:12:40,265
[soft emotional piano music playing]

1139
01:12:53,505 --> 01:12:56,265
- Can you please turn it off?
- Of course, yes. I'm sorry.

1140
01:12:57,025 --> 01:12:58,905
Look, maybe… [sighs]

1141
01:12:58,985 --> 01:13:00,625
I'm just being nosy here…

1142
01:13:02,745 --> 01:13:04,465
But is everything okay at home?

1143
01:13:06,625 --> 01:13:09,105
We're here,
if there's anything we can do for you.

1144
01:13:09,185 --> 01:13:10,625
You know where to find us.

1145
01:13:11,385 --> 01:13:12,425
Okay?

1146
01:13:17,385 --> 01:13:20,625
[Martín] What were you thinking?
Driving with your foot like that?

1147
01:13:21,705 --> 01:13:23,105
You heard the doctor.

1148
01:13:26,065 --> 01:13:28,025
Did you want to get in an accident?

1149
01:13:28,625 --> 01:13:29,785
Want me to lose you too?

1150
01:13:29,865 --> 01:13:31,705
I needed to see for myself.

1151
01:13:32,545 --> 01:13:33,545
See what?

1152
01:13:34,585 --> 01:13:37,505
That your mother had been drinking
until she blacked out?

1153
01:13:37,585 --> 01:13:38,865
You needed to see that?

1154
01:13:39,385 --> 01:13:42,465
That's what I've been trying
to protect you from, sweetheart.

1155
01:13:42,545 --> 01:13:43,825
Seeing moments like that…

1156
01:13:43,905 --> 01:13:45,625
You didn't need to know about it.

1157
01:13:46,225 --> 01:13:48,305
I don't want you
to remember her like that.

1158
01:13:48,825 --> 01:13:49,825
I'm sorry, Dad.

1159
01:13:51,145 --> 01:13:53,065
Promise me you'll take it easy.

1160
01:13:53,665 --> 01:13:55,625
And give your head a rest as well.

1161
01:13:56,505 --> 01:13:58,625
Take a bath, relax. Hm?

1162
01:14:00,225 --> 01:14:02,545
Drink your tea, please, it'll help you.

1163
01:14:05,105 --> 01:14:07,105
[somber music playing]

1164
01:14:21,145 --> 01:14:22,145
[door closing]

1165
01:14:33,305 --> 01:14:34,545
[gasps]

1166
01:14:34,625 --> 01:14:36,865
[unsettling music playing]

1167
01:14:40,625 --> 01:14:43,065
[breathing shakily]

1168
01:14:58,505 --> 01:14:59,825
[banging on door]

1169
01:15:01,465 --> 01:15:03,145
[panting]

1170
01:15:03,225 --> 01:15:05,665
[Martín, faintly]
Ruth, do you need anything?

1171
01:15:07,665 --> 01:15:09,225
[knocking on door]

1172
01:15:09,305 --> 01:15:10,785
[Martín] Honey, are you okay?

1173
01:15:12,385 --> 01:15:13,865
Ruth, say something.

1174
01:15:13,945 --> 01:15:15,425
[banging on door]

1175
01:15:15,505 --> 01:15:17,105
[phone ringing]

1176
01:15:17,185 --> 01:15:19,545
- [Arón on phone] <i>Ruth?</i>
- [banging continues]

1177
01:15:21,385 --> 01:15:22,985
<i>Are you okay? What's wrong?</i>

1178
01:15:23,825 --> 01:15:26,785
She wouldn't open the door,
now she's locked herself in her room.

1179
01:15:36,265 --> 01:15:37,625
[Ruth] It's so fucking weird.

1180
01:15:37,705 --> 01:15:41,185
I was fine. I was fine,
and I drank my dad's fucking herbal tea,

1181
01:15:41,265 --> 01:15:43,025
and then I started to feel dizzy.

1182
01:15:43,505 --> 01:15:44,505
You know?

1183
01:15:44,585 --> 01:15:46,505
- Okay.
- Everything started spinning.

1184
01:15:46,585 --> 01:15:50,065
I was staggering around,
just like my mom was in the videos.

1185
01:15:50,145 --> 01:15:53,825
So now I'm starting to think that maybe
my dad could have given her something.

1186
01:15:53,905 --> 01:15:57,105
Something that his friend the coroner
left off of the fucking report.

1187
01:15:57,185 --> 01:15:59,265
- And that's why it wasn't there.
- Hang on.

1188
01:15:59,345 --> 01:16:01,425
I don't think I'm following all of this.

1189
01:16:02,105 --> 01:16:03,305
And I'm a little high.

1190
01:16:03,385 --> 01:16:06,585
I thought, in the video,
you can see your dad knocking on the door

1191
01:16:06,665 --> 01:16:09,505
- and your mother doesn't answer. She…
- Who told you that?

1192
01:16:10,985 --> 01:16:13,145
Because the video doesn't have any audio.

1193
01:16:13,785 --> 01:16:16,105
And maybe he was doing
this whole performance.

1194
01:16:16,185 --> 01:16:18,145
Banging on the door,
breaking the door down.

1195
01:16:18,225 --> 01:16:20,425
When he sees her in there
and she can't move,

1196
01:16:20,505 --> 01:16:23,385
he knows he's away from the cameras
and kills her in cold blood.

1197
01:16:23,465 --> 01:16:26,385
But, Ruth,
Ruth, are you listening to yourself?

1198
01:16:26,465 --> 01:16:28,625
- Do you hear what you're saying?
- He drugged me.

1199
01:16:28,705 --> 01:16:30,665
He drugged me. I couldn't stand up.

1200
01:16:30,745 --> 01:16:32,705
Look, I don't know. Were you smoking?

1201
01:16:32,785 --> 01:16:36,025
- Or did you have anything else today?
- I didn't smoke anything.

1202
01:16:36,105 --> 01:16:37,225
Nothing.

1203
01:16:37,905 --> 01:16:39,185
A painkiller.

1204
01:16:39,265 --> 01:16:40,425
Just a painkiller.

1205
01:16:41,265 --> 01:16:44,225
One from the doctor
and they gave me another at the station.

1206
01:16:44,305 --> 01:16:45,745
Then maybe that was it.

1207
01:16:47,905 --> 01:16:49,105
You don't believe me?

1208
01:16:49,785 --> 01:16:50,985
Huh?

1209
01:16:51,065 --> 01:16:52,745
I saw you talking to my father.

1210
01:16:53,465 --> 01:16:54,985
I saw you guys chat.

1211
01:16:55,625 --> 01:16:58,145
Your dad's just really worried
about you. I'm sure.

1212
01:16:58,225 --> 01:16:59,345
Are you sure?

1213
01:16:59,825 --> 01:17:00,665
Yes.

1214
01:17:00,745 --> 01:17:01,985
Did he give you a job?

1215
01:17:02,825 --> 01:17:04,105
Look, all right, enough.

1216
01:17:04,625 --> 01:17:06,225
Okay, Ruth. That's enough.

1217
01:17:06,305 --> 01:17:08,465
Next time you think
your dad wants to kill you,

1218
01:17:08,545 --> 01:17:11,425
or he's seeing aliens
or any of that shit, call someone else.

1219
01:17:12,065 --> 01:17:14,065
Because I'm tired of it, Ruth, honestly.

1220
01:17:14,145 --> 01:17:17,945
I have to come running whenever
you want a locksmith, weed, to get laid.

1221
01:17:19,905 --> 01:17:20,905
Yeah.

1222
01:17:22,705 --> 01:17:25,505
So I guess you're the one
who's been taken advantage of.

1223
01:17:26,305 --> 01:17:29,705
Because everything you do is so selfless,
isn't that right, Arón?

1224
01:17:29,785 --> 01:17:31,425
[moody dramatic music playing]

1225
01:17:31,505 --> 01:17:35,065
Not because you think
maybe you and me could get back together.

1226
01:17:36,225 --> 01:17:37,105
[scoffs]

1227
01:17:37,185 --> 01:17:38,465
I'm done, Ruth.

1228
01:17:50,505 --> 01:17:51,945
[door closes]

1229
01:17:55,145 --> 01:17:56,425
[vehicle door opens]

1230
01:17:57,185 --> 01:17:58,545
[vehicle door closes]

1231
01:17:59,145 --> 01:18:00,545
[engine starts]

1232
01:18:06,865 --> 01:18:08,025
[groans faintly]

1233
01:18:11,425 --> 01:18:12,465
[lock clicks]

1234
01:18:26,065 --> 01:18:27,185
[knock on door]

1235
01:18:27,985 --> 01:18:30,705
- [door knob rattles]
- [Martín] Ruth. Open the door.

1236
01:18:30,785 --> 01:18:32,185
[knocking]

1237
01:18:32,265 --> 01:18:33,265
Ruth.

1238
01:18:34,265 --> 01:18:35,265
Are you okay?

1239
01:18:35,985 --> 01:18:38,025
Do you want me to call Dr. Velasco?

1240
01:18:39,705 --> 01:18:41,265
[knocking on door]

1241
01:18:42,985 --> 01:18:44,545
[footsteps receding]

1242
01:18:44,625 --> 01:18:46,745
[moody dramatic music continues]

1243
01:19:00,705 --> 01:19:02,705
[suspenseful music playing]

1244
01:19:27,825 --> 01:19:30,705
ALFONSO ALWAYS MAKES US SMILE

1245
01:19:30,785 --> 01:19:31,785
[door closing]

1246
01:19:34,905 --> 01:19:36,185
[car door opening]

1247
01:19:40,265 --> 01:19:41,425
[car door closing]

1248
01:19:44,225 --> 01:19:45,825
[vehicle engine starts]

1249
01:20:02,985 --> 01:20:04,785
CIVIL GUARD

1250
01:20:25,145 --> 01:20:26,865
I'm here to talk to Sergeant Ballester.

1251
01:20:32,345 --> 01:20:34,065
He's not answering. Wait a moment.

1252
01:20:34,145 --> 01:20:36,545
- I can't wait, it's an emergency.
- What? Hey!

1253
01:20:41,825 --> 01:20:42,825
Hey!

1254
01:20:42,905 --> 01:20:44,785
[music continues]

1255
01:20:52,385 --> 01:20:53,585
[guard] Excuse me.

1256
01:20:54,825 --> 01:20:56,265
You can't be back here.

1257
01:20:56,345 --> 01:20:58,345
[dramatic music playing]

1258
01:21:22,705 --> 01:21:25,065
[suspenseful music playing]

1259
01:21:25,145 --> 01:21:26,665
[knob rattling]

1260
01:21:28,225 --> 01:21:31,385
[grunting]

1261
01:21:45,505 --> 01:21:47,305
[grunting]

1262
01:21:48,665 --> 01:21:49,985
Ruth!

1263
01:21:50,065 --> 01:21:51,225
What are you doing?

1264
01:21:52,545 --> 01:21:53,865
What are you doing, Ruth?

1265
01:21:58,625 --> 01:21:59,785
I'm sorry.

1266
01:22:01,305 --> 01:22:02,785
I'm sorry for everything.

1267
01:22:07,105 --> 01:22:10,225
Can you make me
one of your herbal teas, please, Dad?

1268
01:22:11,105 --> 01:22:12,225
Of course.

1269
01:22:22,785 --> 01:22:24,145
[music stops]

1270
01:22:24,225 --> 01:22:27,265
[breathing shakily]

1271
01:22:28,105 --> 01:22:30,305
[tense foreboding music playing]

1272
01:22:52,105 --> 01:22:53,345
[music fades]

1273
01:23:04,385 --> 01:23:05,945
[cell phone buzzing]

1274
01:23:08,625 --> 01:23:10,825
UNKNOWN NUMBER

1275
01:23:17,265 --> 01:23:19,625
[Ballester] <i>Ruth, this is</i>
<i>Sergeant Ballester.</i>

1276
01:23:20,385 --> 01:23:21,985
<i>They told me you came to see me.</i>

1277
01:23:22,585 --> 01:23:23,705
Yeah.

1278
01:23:24,385 --> 01:23:26,825
<i>You probably saw your father in my office.</i>

1279
01:23:27,865 --> 01:23:29,145
<i>I think we need to talk.</i>

1280
01:23:29,225 --> 01:23:30,225
Because?

1281
01:23:31,025 --> 01:23:34,385
<i>Your father came to see me this morning</i>
<i>to tell me that he thinks…</i>

1282
01:23:35,625 --> 01:23:39,225
<i>that you might be complicit</i>
<i>in your mother's death somehow.</i>

1283
01:23:39,305 --> 01:23:41,185
[melancholy music playing]

1284
01:23:41,265 --> 01:23:43,545
<i>He thinks you have ties</i>
<i>to the pharmaceutical company</i>

1285
01:23:43,625 --> 01:23:45,145
<i>that opened a branch in town.</i>

1286
01:23:45,225 --> 01:23:48,705
<i>He thinks you and others in town</i>
<i>are working together on something.</i>

1287
01:23:49,785 --> 01:23:52,945
<i>He thinks coincidences</i>
<i>don't exist. Anyway…</i>

1288
01:23:53,025 --> 01:23:57,105
<i>Things a person with a lot of free time</i>
<i>would say, someone who feels very alone.</i>

1289
01:23:57,185 --> 01:23:58,825
<i>We've been seeing this a lot.</i>

1290
01:23:59,705 --> 01:24:00,785
STILL LOOKING FOR THE BOY

1291
01:24:00,865 --> 01:24:02,265
ARE THEY INVOLVED?

1292
01:24:02,345 --> 01:24:04,625
<i>I just want you to know, it's our opinion,</i>

1293
01:24:04,705 --> 01:24:07,425
<i>and the opinion of anyone</i>
<i>with even half a brain,</i>

1294
01:24:07,505 --> 01:24:10,145
<i>that the case is closed</i>
<i>and there was no crime.</i>

1295
01:24:11,585 --> 01:24:12,785
It was an accident.

1296
01:24:13,545 --> 01:24:17,145
<i>Every piece of evidence points to it</i>
<i>being a tragic accident, yes.</i>

1297
01:24:23,345 --> 01:24:24,705
Okay, then why?

1298
01:24:25,505 --> 01:24:26,665
<i>Why what?</i>

1299
01:24:30,505 --> 01:24:33,145
But then why invent
this whole crazy story?

1300
01:24:34,225 --> 01:24:38,425
[sighs] <i>Because I'm sure</i>
<i>it's very hard to accept</i>

1301
01:24:38,505 --> 01:24:42,185
<i>that you've lost the person you love</i>
<i>in such a meaningless way.</i>

1302
01:24:43,865 --> 01:24:44,945
[dramatic tone plays]

1303
01:24:50,225 --> 01:24:52,225
It's always easier to blame someone else.

1304
01:24:54,785 --> 01:24:57,545
<i>It's hard to deal with that much pain.</i>

1305
01:24:57,625 --> 01:24:59,945
<i>And I think believing all that</i>

1306
01:25:00,025 --> 01:25:03,505
<i>makes your father feel,</i>
<i>well, special, useful.</i>

1307
01:25:11,305 --> 01:25:13,665
<i>But I just wanted to warn you that…</i>

1308
01:25:14,705 --> 01:25:16,385
<i>your father is a true fanatic.</i>

1309
01:25:16,465 --> 01:25:18,185
<i>And a fanatic is…</i>

1310
01:25:19,145 --> 01:25:20,585
<i>incredibly dangerous.</i>

1311
01:25:30,465 --> 01:25:32,265
<i>Be careful with him, please.</i>

1312
01:25:34,305 --> 01:25:35,265
Thank you.

1313
01:25:38,625 --> 01:25:39,905
[phone clicks]

1314
01:25:39,985 --> 01:25:41,105
[music fades]

1315
01:25:43,105 --> 01:25:44,025
[gasps faintly]

1316
01:25:49,065 --> 01:25:50,065
Hey, Dad?

1317
01:25:53,745 --> 01:25:54,785
Dad?

1318
01:25:55,905 --> 01:25:57,145
- I'm sorry.
- Hey, hey!

1319
01:25:57,225 --> 01:25:58,825
- I'm so sorry.
- Stay right there.

1320
01:25:58,905 --> 01:25:59,825
I just wanna talk.

1321
01:25:59,905 --> 01:26:02,025
[Berta] You almost killed him,
for God's sake.

1322
01:26:02,625 --> 01:26:04,905
Let me explain.
It was… it was a mistake. I didn't…

1323
01:26:06,265 --> 01:26:09,265
- Dad.
- Can't you see he's scared of you?

1324
01:26:09,985 --> 01:26:11,105
With good reason.

1325
01:26:12,305 --> 01:26:14,905
- Oh, Dad, come on.
- All right, give me your hands.

1326
01:26:15,985 --> 01:26:18,385
If you wanna talk to your father,
we have to tie your hands.

1327
01:26:18,465 --> 01:26:21,905
- What are you talking about? How…
- We can't risk you attacking him again.

1328
01:26:21,985 --> 01:26:23,505
Come on, please.

1329
01:26:28,705 --> 01:26:30,185
Do what she says, sweetheart.

1330
01:26:41,025 --> 01:26:42,985
[zip tie tightening]

1331
01:26:44,305 --> 01:26:47,425
It's because I was convinced
you had something to do with Mom's death.

1332
01:26:48,625 --> 01:26:50,745
I'm sorry. I'm sorry, Dad.

1333
01:26:50,825 --> 01:26:52,825
I didn't realize. I don't…

1334
01:26:52,905 --> 01:26:54,705
I didn't mean to hurt you, really.

1335
01:26:54,785 --> 01:26:55,785
[sighs]

1336
01:26:57,985 --> 01:26:59,945
What… What are you going to do?

1337
01:27:00,025 --> 01:27:01,865
Are you gonna call the Civil Guard?

1338
01:27:04,345 --> 01:27:05,305
What happens now?

1339
01:27:06,185 --> 01:27:07,945
[Ruth] Ow. Oh, fuck!

1340
01:27:08,025 --> 01:27:09,785
Is this really necessary?

1341
01:27:13,265 --> 01:27:14,305
Come here.

1342
01:27:19,865 --> 01:27:22,425
Your daughter's becoming
a problem for us, Martín.

1343
01:27:23,105 --> 01:27:25,945
We have no idea what she saw
when she was in the attic.

1344
01:27:28,225 --> 01:27:29,465
We have to act fast.

1345
01:27:30,345 --> 01:27:33,145
We have to go to the tunnels now
to find the children.

1346
01:27:33,225 --> 01:27:34,905
[vehicle approaching]

1347
01:27:34,985 --> 01:27:37,305
- [vehicle horn honking]
- [gasps faintly]

1348
01:27:37,385 --> 01:27:39,385
[suspenseful music playing]

1349
01:27:41,305 --> 01:27:42,825
[Berta] Who the fuck is that?

1350
01:27:42,905 --> 01:27:45,265
[Martín] The doctor.
He must have come to see Ruth.

1351
01:27:46,225 --> 01:27:49,025
- [Berta] Get him out of here.
- What are you gonna do with that?

1352
01:27:49,785 --> 01:27:51,105
Is that Arón's pass?

1353
01:27:55,705 --> 01:27:57,785
Please, Dad! No. No, Dad!

1354
01:27:58,385 --> 01:28:00,905
- Please, don't leave me here!
- Stay quiet, Jesus Christ.

1355
01:28:00,985 --> 01:28:02,345
- Dad!
- [TV volume increases]

1356
01:28:02,425 --> 01:28:04,305
[yelling] I need help!

1357
01:28:04,385 --> 01:28:05,665
Would you quit that?

1358
01:28:05,745 --> 01:28:07,185
- Dad! Dad! No.
- Be quiet.

1359
01:28:07,265 --> 01:28:09,665
[muffled yelling]

1360
01:28:14,025 --> 01:28:15,665
[woman on TV] <i>Emma, make your guess.</i>

1361
01:28:15,745 --> 01:28:19,065
- [muffled screaming]
- Let's all just relax a little bit, hmm?

1362
01:28:20,025 --> 01:28:21,305
[car engine starts]

1363
01:28:21,385 --> 01:28:24,305
Hey. Calm down, calm down.
It's been dealt with.

1364
01:28:28,545 --> 01:28:29,705
I'll be in the car.

1365
01:28:29,785 --> 01:28:32,225
- [TV continues indistinctly]
- [Ruth whimpers]

1366
01:28:34,185 --> 01:28:35,305
[mutes TV]

1367
01:28:43,785 --> 01:28:44,785
[coughs]

1368
01:28:46,305 --> 01:28:48,825
Dad, what are you
going to do with Arón's key card?

1369
01:28:50,345 --> 01:28:52,385
Did Mom find out what you're working on?

1370
01:28:53,025 --> 01:28:55,345
Is that why you deleted
everything on her phone?

1371
01:28:56,105 --> 01:28:59,985
Your mother went into the attic, as well.
She found out about what we're gonna do.

1372
01:29:00,505 --> 01:29:01,825
And she lost it.

1373
01:29:02,425 --> 01:29:04,585
After that, she started
drinking more than normal.

1374
01:29:04,665 --> 01:29:07,665
But I think her death was no accident.

1375
01:29:07,745 --> 01:29:09,385
Somebody killed her.

1376
01:29:11,025 --> 01:29:13,265
You really believe that
I was involved somehow?

1377
01:29:13,345 --> 01:29:15,985
I know you would never
try to hurt your mother.

1378
01:29:16,585 --> 01:29:18,465
But they could have used you to frame me,

1379
01:29:18,545 --> 01:29:21,385
to implicate me in her death
and get me out of the way.

1380
01:29:21,985 --> 01:29:24,345
I didn't think so at first, but all this,

1381
01:29:24,425 --> 01:29:27,385
your mother's death
so soon after security caught me

1382
01:29:27,465 --> 01:29:29,625
investigating around their main facility.

1383
01:29:30,105 --> 01:29:31,425
Too much of a coincidence.

1384
01:29:32,185 --> 01:29:33,185
Mom fell down.

1385
01:29:33,945 --> 01:29:36,545
I know it's so hard to accept,
but we have no choice.

1386
01:29:37,305 --> 01:29:40,105
What happened was horrible
but it doesn't help to invent stories.

1387
01:29:40,185 --> 01:29:43,265
I told Dr. Velasco you were asleep
and to come back later this afternoon.

1388
01:29:43,825 --> 01:29:44,745
He'll untie you.

1389
01:29:49,745 --> 01:29:50,665
Dad?

1390
01:29:51,465 --> 01:29:52,385
Come back!

1391
01:29:53,145 --> 01:29:54,145
Oh, shit.

1392
01:29:55,545 --> 01:29:56,825
[car door opens]

1393
01:29:57,985 --> 01:29:59,825
[somber music playing]

1394
01:30:06,345 --> 01:30:07,625
[vehicle engine starts]

1395
01:30:11,825 --> 01:30:12,825
Shit.

1396
01:30:18,545 --> 01:30:21,585
[Ruth yelling] Help! Help!

1397
01:30:22,385 --> 01:30:23,425
[grunting]

1398
01:30:29,985 --> 01:30:33,145
ALEJANDRO HUELVA, MISSING.

1399
01:30:34,065 --> 01:30:35,145
[dramatic tone plays]

1400
01:30:40,745 --> 01:30:41,945
He's in the lake.

1401
01:30:43,305 --> 01:30:45,305
[tense music playing]

1402
01:30:48,425 --> 01:30:49,505
[grunting]

1403
01:30:51,545 --> 01:30:53,225
[panting]

1404
01:30:53,305 --> 01:30:54,625
[groaning]

1405
01:31:04,985 --> 01:31:05,945
[grunts]

1406
01:31:10,105 --> 01:31:11,345
[grunting]

1407
01:31:12,185 --> 01:31:13,145
Ah!

1408
01:31:14,065 --> 01:31:15,225
[groaning]

1409
01:31:18,185 --> 01:31:20,185
[tense music continues]

1410
01:31:22,585 --> 01:31:23,545
[phone beeps]

1411
01:31:23,625 --> 01:31:25,345
[line ringing]

1412
01:31:29,345 --> 01:31:30,505
[groaning softly]

1413
01:31:36,065 --> 01:31:37,865
Arón, you have to listen to me.

1414
01:31:38,745 --> 01:31:41,785
<i>I know I've said</i>
<i>a lot of really stupid things lately.</i>

1415
01:31:41,865 --> 01:31:44,225
<i>I know. But I promise</i>
<i>that this is serious.</i>

1416
01:31:44,305 --> 01:31:47,985
<i>My father has your pass and he's going</i>
<i>to use it to get into the building.</i>

1417
01:31:48,785 --> 01:31:50,425
<i>He might already be inside.</i>

1418
01:31:50,505 --> 01:31:52,985
<i>And that's not all.</i>
<i>He has a gun and explosives.</i>

1419
01:31:53,065 --> 01:31:55,505
<i>That woman, the one with him,</i>
<i>she was in the army.</i>

1420
01:31:55,585 --> 01:31:58,505
<i>She knows how to use them.</i>
<i>They're headed to the basement.</i>

1421
01:31:58,585 --> 01:32:01,945
<i>They think that there are children</i>
<i>hidden in secret tunnels or…</i>

1422
01:32:02,025 --> 01:32:03,465
<i>I don't know.</i>

1423
01:32:03,545 --> 01:32:05,785
<i>God knows what</i>
<i>they're willing to do to find them.</i>

1424
01:32:05,865 --> 01:32:06,905
<i>I'm on my way, okay?</i>

1425
01:32:06,985 --> 01:32:09,425
<i>But I might not get there in time.</i>

1426
01:32:09,505 --> 01:32:11,625
<i>I need you to believe me, please, Arón.</i>

1427
01:32:11,705 --> 01:32:12,745
<i>Please.</i>

1428
01:32:12,825 --> 01:32:14,185
[cell phone beeps]

1429
01:32:14,265 --> 01:32:17,185
[Ruth] <i>…and he's going to use it</i>
<i>to get into the building.</i>

1430
01:32:17,265 --> 01:32:18,985
<i>He might already be inside.</i>

1431
01:32:19,065 --> 01:32:22,065
<i>And that's not all.</i>
<i>He has a gun and explosives.</i>

1432
01:32:24,505 --> 01:32:25,505
Careful.

1433
01:32:27,345 --> 01:32:29,425
[intense music playing]

1434
01:32:37,505 --> 01:32:38,745
[Martín] Wait.

1435
01:32:41,185 --> 01:32:42,305
Let's go.

1436
01:32:48,505 --> 01:32:50,865
[alarm blaring]

1437
01:32:53,265 --> 01:32:54,345
I'll go.

1438
01:33:00,385 --> 01:33:02,185
[alarm continues]

1439
01:33:09,585 --> 01:33:10,585
[Arón grunting]

1440
01:33:25,505 --> 01:33:26,585
[Berta] All good.

1441
01:33:27,985 --> 01:33:29,025
Let's go.

1442
01:33:36,465 --> 01:33:38,505
[alarm continues ringing]

1443
01:33:40,705 --> 01:33:42,105
[beeping]

1444
01:33:53,545 --> 01:33:55,305
[man] Hey! Stop, you can't go in!

1445
01:33:55,385 --> 01:33:57,745
My dad's inside. Let me in the building.

1446
01:33:57,825 --> 01:34:00,185
- [loud explosion]
- [people screaming]

1447
01:34:15,545 --> 01:34:18,625
Arón. Hey, hey, are you all right?

1448
01:34:19,705 --> 01:34:20,785
[explosion]

1449
01:34:24,065 --> 01:34:25,545
[explosion]

1450
01:34:25,625 --> 01:34:27,225
[coughing]

1451
01:34:28,465 --> 01:34:30,905
We have to keep looking.
They must be behind the next wall.

1452
01:34:30,985 --> 01:34:32,505
They have to be back there.

1453
01:34:34,265 --> 01:34:36,025
[alarm ringing]

1454
01:34:41,385 --> 01:34:42,705
Dad, wait.

1455
01:34:42,785 --> 01:34:44,825
Ruth. What… what are you doing here?

1456
01:34:44,905 --> 01:34:47,105
- Go home.
- No. No, you have to come with me.

1457
01:34:47,985 --> 01:34:48,985
I can't, sweetheart.

1458
01:34:49,065 --> 01:34:50,425
The boy isn't here.

1459
01:34:50,505 --> 01:34:52,905
Remember when I cut myself
on metal in the lake?

1460
01:34:52,985 --> 01:34:55,105
It was his bike, it was Alejandro's.

1461
01:34:55,185 --> 01:34:58,145
He drowned in there.
He fell in with his bike, I'm sure of it.

1462
01:34:58,985 --> 01:35:01,985
You would say anything if you thought
it would make me leave with you.

1463
01:35:02,065 --> 01:35:05,105
No, Dad, really. If you don't believe me,
we'll go to the police.

1464
01:35:05,185 --> 01:35:07,505
I'm sure they'll find him.
He's in the lake.

1465
01:35:07,585 --> 01:35:11,065
Even if that were true,
it's not just Alejandro.

1466
01:35:11,145 --> 01:35:15,465
It's all the other children too. The kids
they've taken and the kids they will take.

1467
01:35:15,545 --> 01:35:18,425
Don't you see that we can save
them all by exposing the truth?

1468
01:35:18,505 --> 01:35:22,225
I understand, I do,
but you don't need to save anyone.

1469
01:35:22,305 --> 01:35:23,985
You don't need to save any child.

1470
01:35:24,065 --> 01:35:27,185
You don't need to be the one
to change the world or be a hero.

1471
01:35:28,465 --> 01:35:30,305
Isn't it enough to be my dad?

1472
01:35:31,105 --> 01:35:32,585
Because I really do need you.

1473
01:35:34,225 --> 01:35:37,345
Come with me. We'll make another plan,
we'll come back another day.

1474
01:35:37,425 --> 01:35:39,585
Whatever you want.
I'm not leaving you again.

1475
01:35:39,665 --> 01:35:42,385
I'm never leaving your side again,
I swear, Dad, I promise.

1476
01:35:42,465 --> 01:35:44,545
[loud rumbling]

1477
01:35:44,625 --> 01:35:45,665
I love you.

1478
01:35:49,945 --> 01:35:52,545
- Ruth. Let's go!
- No, no! Come back!

1479
01:35:52,625 --> 01:35:55,105
- [Arón] Let's get outta here!
- [Ruth] Dad!

1480
01:35:56,665 --> 01:35:58,065
[loud rumbling]

1481
01:36:05,065 --> 01:36:08,185
- [alarm ringing]
- [coughing]

1482
01:36:12,025 --> 01:36:14,025
[tense music fades]

1483
01:36:14,105 --> 01:36:16,065
[distant siren blaring]

1484
01:36:18,105 --> 01:36:20,745
[emotional music playing]

1485
01:36:20,825 --> 01:36:23,185
[sobbing]

1486
01:36:40,065 --> 01:36:42,065
[music continues]

1487
01:36:44,265 --> 01:36:47,505
[man on radio] <i>Today we woke up</i>
<i>to the sad news that Alejandro Huelva,</i>

1488
01:36:47,585 --> 01:36:49,985
<i>the nine-year-old boy</i>
<i>who went missing a month ago,</i>

1489
01:36:50,065 --> 01:36:52,345
<i>has been found dead in Lake Caldas.</i>

1490
01:36:52,425 --> 01:36:54,985
<i>His body was discovered</i>
<i>trapped under debris.</i>

1491
01:36:55,065 --> 01:36:59,425
<i>A tragedy that adds to the deaths last</i>
<i>week of Martín Arce and Berta Martínez,</i>

1492
01:36:59,505 --> 01:37:02,145
<i>who were killed in the explosions</i>
<i>they themselves caused</i>

1493
01:37:02,225 --> 01:37:05,305
<i>at the headquarters</i>
<i>of the pharmaceutical company Certyx.</i>

1494
01:37:05,385 --> 01:37:08,025
<i>Reports indicate that</i>
<i>the Arce and Martinez believed</i>

1495
01:37:08,105 --> 01:37:10,865
<i>there were hidden tunnels</i>
<i>connected to the basement of the building</i>

1496
01:37:10,945 --> 01:37:13,385
<i>where they would find Alejandro</i>
<i>and other missing children,</i>

1497
01:37:13,465 --> 01:37:17,185
<i>kidnapped, they believed, by the company,</i>
<i>a conspiracy theory that gained traction--</i>

1498
01:37:17,265 --> 01:37:18,345
[radio turns off]

1499
01:37:18,425 --> 01:37:20,425
[emotional music continues]

1500
01:37:34,865 --> 01:37:36,905
